{:task "seon.proofs/one-prove-path", :plan "Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md, root's special cases go: one prove path). What existed: a proof run's tick budget was per run, two keys by mode (`:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` for the judge, a turn's own 10x smaller one), so a test's verdict depended on where it ran and on what ran before it in the run: a proof that needed more than the turn's budget was red (`:seon.test/ticks-spent` on the running test) in every turn and green at the judge, and a cheap test after a costly one was red in one run and green alone. bin/test had no bound at all, and its greens are reused by digest by agents. And the judge forked a child even when every test it selected was green at its digest (what the turn had just run). Changed: `seon.proofs/run-tests!` (the one runner) takes a per-test bound: each test, its :each fixtures included, runs under a Jolt `run-interruptible` budget of its own inside its :each fixtures (so their teardown runs), the namespace's fixtures within the tests' budgets and one more (`seon.proofs/test-vars`, Jolt's clojure.test fixture atoms); a test past it is an `:error` report of `:seon.test/ticks-spent` naming it and the run goes on; each result records its `:ticks`. One key, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (200e9, the old judge bound), per test, used by a turn, the judge and bin/test (test/test_runner.clj); 0 means none, read once in the runner. `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` forks nothing when nothing is left to run. Deleted: the turn's own key (config, two schema entries), the mode switch in `seon.agent/handle!`, `seon.agent/run-tests-here`'s run-level budget and its error assembly. Kept by ruling (a), 2026-10-22: what a turn selects (proofs and what the branch changed) and what the judge selects (the plan); the judge runs the plan less every green at its digest, so after a turn it runs only what the turn did not. Not changed: reds are not reused (a red reruns, to the same verdict now that the bound is the test's own); the judge still asks the agent's `:tests` op rather than `seon.prove` (its runs are not recorded on main as the turn's are). Correctness: one verdict per test and digest everywhere. Perf: a judge after a covering turn forks no child and runs no test (measured in seon.agent-test/one-bound-one-verdict-and-the-judge-reruns-no-green: :ran 0, no :pid); a turn may now do up to the per-test bound per selected test (its selection is the proofs and what changed).", :changed #{"docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "docs/processes.md#the-agent-process" "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches" "seon.proofs/run-tests!" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "seon.agent/handle!" "seon.agent/test-report" "docs/turn.md#observe" "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/turn.md#who-runs-which-tests" "docs/failure-points.md#entrant-boundaries-a-defect-becomes-a-recorded-error-map" "seon.root/run-tests!" ":seon.config.agent/test-ticks" "seon.agent/run-tests-here" "seon.proofs/test-vars"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 34%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check. The merge's proof is `bin/prove` on the merged tree, then `bin/check smoke`; `bin/laws` (every law's sites) and `health` ([health](health.md)) run inside it as steps keyed by the whole tree. One rule selects for everyone: a key runs exactly when no green result is stored at its reach digest (```seon.proofs/plan```), in the one results directory every checkout shares (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`), so an unchanged tree runs nothing and a result proven in any checkout is reused in every other." "docs/merging.md#the-test-service 25%: One proc, `:prove` (```seon.prove/prove-proc```, declared in `resources/seon/system.edn`), admits every request to run tests, whoever asks: a Claude session (the server's `:prove` op, the MCP tool `seon_prove`, ```seon.root/prove!```) and an agent's turn (```seon.agent/prove!```, which ```seon.turn/observe``` calls) share it, so a change is proved once." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 22%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`). ```seon.program/reach-digests``` gives each test of a db its reach digest: its own form, its namespace's forms that define no var (the ns form, fixtures), and every live form their `:form/refs` reach (a var to the form that last defined it; a defined multimethod's methods and protocol's extensions), and every schema entry a reached var's contract names (and each entry the entry's form names, on), each form and entry digested once; plus the checkout's inputs with no program facts (`seon.fx/inputs`: deps.edn, bin/, resources/ with the schemas contracts name, test fixtures and every submodule pin, Jolt's included; not task files or prose; and the per-test tick bound), plus the values of the config keys its reach reads (```seon.program/config-reads```: the `seon.config.*` keys its reached forms name, every key for one referencing the config map from outside `seon.config`, such as root's boot; their values from the config in force, ```seon.fx/config-input```: the shipped file, the `--config` layers the command line names, the `--set` overrides and the `#env` values under the profile, with the checkout's path normalized and the place keys left out), plus the vars data names that root resolves by name (`seon.fx/declared-refs`: system.edn's procs, edges of `seon.system/read-decl`), and no tree: a test whose reached forms call a file reader or run the binary (`seon.program/tree-readers`; a test runs target/seon, bin/root or bin/check only through `seon.test-store/binary-sh`) is digested by what it reaches like any other (owner, 2026-10-27: \"no tests should include all of src\"; with the tree it was 286 tests rerun on any edit), and a file or a binary change outside its reach is found by the full run (`bin/test --all`, below). A check step whose reach reads the tree still takes it. A test's digest moves exactly when a form or schema entry it reaches does. A literal name a form passes to `resolve`, `ns-resolve` or `requiring-resolve` is a reference (`seon.program/resolved`): the var it looks up at run time is one it reaches, so a test that resolves its var by name is no green reused after that var changes, or once it comes to exist. Any other name a form holds as data (quoted: `tree-readers`, `env-readers`, `seon.config/schema-forms`, whose `:seon.render/*` properties name every render, a form sent to an agent) is no reference, so reaching that form is neither using nor reaching what it names (below)." "docs/decisions.md#open-owner-questions-pending-rulings-the-plans-blockers-are-also-in-planmd 21%: The questions waiting on the owner's ruling, the plan's blockers among them." "docs/failure-points.md#entrant-boundaries-a-defect-becomes-a-recorded-error-map 10%: The boundaries where a defect in a request becomes a recorded error map instead of ending its process."], :functions [["seon.agent/test-plan 6%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] "seon.proofs/report 6%: clojure.test's own report as this namespace loads (no binding in force), so a run nested in a test's run reports through it, never through the enclosing run's binding." "seon.proofs/*digests* 5%: {test var -> reach digest} of the run-tests! run in progress (bound around each namespace's run), read by test-seed: a generative test draws from its own digest." ["seon.contract/on? 5%: Are the contracts on in this process (:seon.config.contract/instrument)? :off: no wrapper and no recording; :contracts: wrappers, no recording transaction (for measuring the two apart); :on: both." [:seon.config.contract/instrument]] ["seon.agent/test-report 4%: One clojure.test report as the run keeps it: its type, test var and message; a failing assertion's expected and actual printed by the one printer under :part; a cost proof's rows and verdict (seon.cost/proof's :actual) kept as data under :cost (session W2's agent page renders them; the judge persists them on the run); a typed error's :seon.error/type, :ticks and :budget (a test past its tick budget)." [:seon.cost/report :seon.error/type]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "50%"] [:seon.test/ticks-spent "35%"] [:seon.config.proofs/jobs "4%"] [:seon.config.source/test-results-dir "2%"] [:seon.task/state "2%"]]}}
{:task "seon.turn/context-render-observes-what-changed", :plan "THE PLAN step 3 (the agent's turn and its context). `seon.turn/observe` called `seon.reconcile/index` on the branch's db every turn: the whole index listed from empty and settled over every task, though `seon.reconcile/done-input` reads only the agent's task's proofs and kind and the claimants of the tests that ran (notes/perf-2026-10-24.md, Next wins 1: 74% of a 115M-tick, 512 MB context render). Its cost also grew as n^1.75 with the number of tasks. Fix at the cause, with the one incremental mechanism that exists, `seon.reconcile/observe`: `seon.reconcile/index` takes the index of an earlier db and observes the later one over the datoms of the watched attributes that differ (`datahike.api/datoms-diff` per attribute range, which skips the stored subtrees both share), and `seon.turn/index!` holds the agent's last index and db in `seon.agent/state` (:index), so only the first turn lists the index. Deletes the per-turn listing; no new mechanism. Measured on a root's main of 680 tasks and 118k datoms: listing 92.0M ticks and 384 MB, observing 1.29M ticks and 10.2 MB, with equal indexes. The cost proof runs on the in-memory test store, where no node has an address and each diff walks its attribute's datoms, so it holds the linear bound and never the stored db's skip of shared subtrees (measured above, once, by hand). Not taken here: the first turn still lists the index (the fork server could hand main's index to the agents it forks, as it hands :graph).", :changed #{"seon.reconcile/index" "seon.turn/observe" "docs/turn.md#observe" "seon.turn/index!"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/turn.md#observe 67%: Each turn starts from facts measured now, never remembered: `seon.turn/observe` asks the test service (`seon.agent/prove!`, `seon.prove`: one run per branch and tx whoever asks, in a fork of the agent) to run the task's accumulated proofs and the tests the branch changed, each test bounded by `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (a run that cannot be made ran no proof, so every proof is unknown and the run's error is `:test-run`), the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`, the question root's judge asks), its unmerged entries, the problems its own work made true (`seon.problems/caused`: it refuses done), and the done rule over them (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`: [tasks](tasks.md#the-done-rule)), read from the reconciler's index of the branch, which the agent holds across turns and observes from the last turn's db over what differs (`seon.turn/index!`, `seon.reconcile/index`): listing it again every turn cost 92M ticks and 384 MB on a main of 680 tasks, observing it 1.3M and 10 MB. That is all a turn observes: the done rule's inputs. What else a reader wants of the branch (whether it can merge, what its merge carries, what it changed under the wiki, its tests) is a view of the task's render (```seon.render.ui/merge-view```, `divergence-view`, `caused-view`, `gaps-view`), a component over the branch's db with main's and the merge base's commits as its children, drawn when one of them moves, never per turn (owner, 2026-10-27: \"Info that is derived and made for the agent or the user should go into the hiccup\"). Merge readiness per turn read main's whole diff (1.78B ticks with main moved); a turn's observe now costs 9.4M ticks (measured on an outside agent, nothing new), and an unrelated eval redraws none of the views (`seon.render-test/an-unrelated-change-redraws-none-of-the-merge-views`)." "docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 14%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/ctx.md#kept-by-the-change-never-rebuilt 9%: ``seon.ctx/build`` walks the pattern once, when the agent's state first holds its db (a spawn, a resume, a clone), and ``seon.ctx/patch`` keeps it from each transaction's datoms, which ``seon.agent/transact!`` records with the db they made." "docs/merging.md#the-test-service 8%: One proc, `:prove` (```seon.prove/prove-proc```, declared in `resources/seon/system.edn`), admits every request to run tests, whoever asks: a Claude session (the server's `:prove` op, the MCP tool `seon_prove`, ```seon.root/prove!```) and an agent's turn (```seon.agent/prove!```, which ```seon.turn/observe``` calls) share it, so a change is proved once." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 8%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)."], :functions ["seon.reconcile/empty-index 7%: The index of a db with no tasks." "seon.fork-server/index-at-fork 7%: Server state st with main's reconciler index (:index {:db :index}, listed when the server starts) observed forward to :db (seon.reconcile/index over what differs), when main moved since: a forked agent's first turn observes its branch from it, never lists the index itself (a listing of 903 tasks: 33M ticks, 160 MB, in every agent)." "seon.reconcile/watched-ns 6%" ["seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc 5%: The reconciler." [:seon.run/status :seon.step/step-fn]] ["seon.agent/init! 5%: Make this process the agent: its socket (fd), its namespace, its state map, its interrupt handlers." [:seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable :seon.lib/fd]]], :schemas [[:seon.reconcile/done-input "5%"] [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "3%"] [:seon.task/task-fact "2%"] [:seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes "2%"] [:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "2%"]]}}
{:task "datahike.query/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error", :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (branch jolt/nested-call-refused) under task a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused: datahike.query/filter-by-pred wraps each predicate in a local safe-pred fn, which catches ClassCastException and IllegalArgumentException and answers false, so a predicate given a value of the wrong type ([(clojure.string/ends-with? ?n \"x\")] over symbols, a comparison of a string with a number) drops the row without a word, and the query reads as a true empty answer. A swallowed error (AGENTS section 9): what Datomic does (the query throws), whether any Datahike behaviour or test relies on the false (grep its tests for type-mixed predicates), then the fix at the producer: the error raised with the clause and the offending values, or, if a mixed-type column must filter, a typed comparison that never throws. Proof: a datahike.query-test test whose predicate meets a wrong-typed value raises, naming the clause. Fixed 2026-10-23 (worktree session store, Datahike jolt/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error 6b668f0, 25cc875, 698a2fa): both catch sites, filter-by-pred's safe-pred and the planner's post-filter-preds, now raise :query/call-threw (datahike.query.analyze/call-threw) with the clause, the call's arguments and the cause; a typed error the call threw passes on as is; the fused direct-rel, columnar and secondary-index paths, which caught every Exception as \"not applicable\" and fell back, rethrow it (datahike.query.analyze/call-threw?). Seon's process tests (seon.agent-test, seon.root-test, seon.repl-test, seon.main-test) pass, so no Seon query leaned on the false. Datahike's own query tests were run once on 6b668f0 and failed the same 11 as on d89be47; they were not re-run on 25cc875, because datahike.test.utils needs datahike.index.hitchhiker-tree, which is not on Jolt's source roots. The catch had also covered the built-in comparisons: their CollectionOrder protocol dispatched on the first value's type. Root's ruling (2026-10-23): they keep Clojure's semantics, so (< 1 \"a\") is the query's error, not a quiet answer. Datahike 698a2fa compares a number or nil as clojure.core/< does and any other value with compare. The planner pushes an ordering comparison into a range scan only when the store's schema is written and the bound fits the attribute's :db/valueType (datahike.query.analyze/of-the-values-kind?), so the scan, which would drop a value of another kind, answers what the predicate answers: [(> ?n 5)] over :ns/name symbols is now an AEVT scan whose filter raises, and [(> ?n proof.one-test)] stays an AVET range. On Jolt, clojure.string/ends-with? accepts a symbol (it reads its string), so the proof's predicate is pos? on a symbol.", :changed #{"docs/forks.md#datahike"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/forks.md#datahike 68%: `reference-code/datahike` pinned at `a9df162d`, the tip of ``jolt/own-suites-on-jolt`` (tests only: `a989ac4a` the core suites load on Jolt, `datahike.test.utils`, `api-test` and `core-test` requiring the hitchhiker tree and `kaocha.stacktrace` only on the JVM; `a9df162d` the tests our commits left stale: `query-cache-test` reads a failed call's message as the cause `6b668f0` wraps, `versioning-test` reads the roster through `branches` (`c0f9149`) and names a transaction id past every branch's (`be25be4`), `gc-test` counts the roster's two records, `query-ir-test` expects Jolt's per-cursor merge; [own suites](#own-suites)), on `e8a2999d`, the tip of ``jolt/attr-compare-mixed-kinds`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares two attributes of one kind, keywords, entity ids or strings, with no `try`, and any other pair through `safe-compare`: a variable attribute bound to a string, long or symbol is a slice bound no stored attribute compares with, which since `cc614f4e` threw `ClassCastException` where it had joined nothing, and Seon's `[?f ?a ?s]` with `?a` an input in `seon.docs` reaches it. Proof: `datahike.test.query-planner-test/test-variable-attribute-bound-upstream-reads-bound-slices`, red since `cc614f4e`, green; 0.7 B a keyword comparison. Generic), on `219f57f6`, the tip of ``jolt/diff-test-on-jolt-file-store`` (Datahike's `datoms_diff_test` requires `konserve.jolt.store`, so its restored histories run on Jolt's `:file` backend: without it every restored history threw on konserve's JVM store and the property reported the throw as a counterexample; the diff was exact; test only), on `416bdc63`, the tip of ``jolt/a-diff-records-its-reads`` (`65dad766`: `psi/diff` records its `[from to]` range as `-slice` does, so a read built on `d/datoms-diff` under `d/with-reads` is touched by a datom added in the diffed range: `[:a attr]`, or `:any` with no attribute; it recorded nothing, and a render over a diff kept its old answer. Proofs: `datahike.test.datoms-diff-test/a-diff-records-the-range-it-read`, `seon.render-test/a-render-over-a-diff-runs-again-when-the-diff-moves`. Generic), merged with `0d6b58c0`, the tip of ``jolt/compare-value-no-try`` (`d4811ddf`, `0d6b58c0`: `compare-value` on Jolt enters no `try` for two values of one scalar kind, 240 B a comparison that reaches the value before; `compare-data`'s helpers are top-level fns: n refs on one form 38 to 17 KB a datom at 1,000, `seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts`; a type test beside the `try` until Jolt's `try` costs nothing entered, task jolt.backend/a-try-entered-allocates-nothing), on `cc614f4e`, the tip of ``jolt/attribute-compare-without-try`` (`cmp-attr-nil` compares attributes with no `try`), on `e5a55664`, the tip of ``jolt/time-pred-order``, on `872bfa03`, the tip of ``jolt/pull-reverse``, on `336de2ab`, the tip of ``jolt/tx-data-retracts``, on `3fe48c96` (``jolt/multival-lookup-ref``), on `6dc9ce64`, the tip of ``jolt/with-reads`` (`6689077e` records the index ranges a read scanned: `with-reads`, `reads-index`, `datom-keys`, `reads-touched?`; `6dc9ce64` the collector is an atom), on `698a2fa`, the tip of ``jolt/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error`` (`6b668f0`, `25cc875`, `698a2fa`), on `d89be47` (the tip of ``jolt/datom-cost``: `6db7188` and ``jolt/nested-call-refused`` (`ae35add`, `dda24c0`), each on ``jolt/release-drains`` `bae7c3e` (``jolt/in-scalar`` `1880fcc` merged into it; both on ``jolt/global-ids`` `b4596cf`, ``jolt/gc-cache-epoch`` `700c473` merged in))." "docs/forks.md#jolt 11%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 10%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 3%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check." "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data 3%: The failure points met without a catch, where the edge answers with data."], :functions [["seon.server/ops 7%: The server's ops, the one table, keyed by a request's :op (an EDN request is a keyword-keyed map, {:op :agents}; a line opening `{\"` is MCP's, respond): each op's description, its arguments (JSON Schema properties; :required, the ones it needs) and its answer, a fn of root's system, the agent the request names (:root, an agent's keyword, or nil) and the request." [:seon.error/message :seon.error/type :seon.run/agent :seon.run/id :seon.run/status :seon.run/task]] ["seon.agent/test-plan 5%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.context/refused 5%: The done rule's reasons as the agent reads them, beyond its own red proofs (shown with the proofs): a red test names its failures (a stray one the form that makes it a proof), a contract gap its var, a design step that has not improved its plan the call that does, an open problem its work made true what it is (seon.problems/caused)." [:seon.reconcile/not-done :seon.task/plan :seon.task/proofs]] "seon.program/catches 5%: A :try node's catch clauses, in order, each {:class name (\"*\" for a catch-all) :answers kind}: read from Jolt's lowering of the clauses (one dispatch: an if per class, a catch-all's handler last, else a rethrow)." "seon.contract/checked 5%: f checked by contract form c (compiled once, here: seon.contract/wrap), errors named fn-sym; f itself when instrumentation is off."], :schemas [[:seon.root/no-source-root "3%"] [:seon.root/not-held "2%"] [:seon.error/class "2%"] [:seon.agent/eval-error "2%"] [:seon.root/quiet-before-true "2%"]]}}
{:task "jolt.backend/image-registration-is-opt-in", :plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; Jolt jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in 686aff7d, eba8826a). Jolt registers every fn literal's source for its state image: fnsrc-flush renders the source, reads it back to check it (fnsrc-row-src), emits image-register-fn-form! per literal and wraps the literal in a maker. Seon writes no image. Now an explicit Jolt compile option, not a changed default: *compiler-options* {:image-fn-forms false} makes a top-level form collect no registrations (backend_scheme.clj fnsrc-regs answers nil), its literals emitted as plain closures with their names; absent or true, Jolt is unchanged for every other user. Jolt reads JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS (an EDN map) into *compiler-options*' root at start (post-prelude.ss), as JVM Clojure reads the clojure.compiler.* properties, and Seon sets it in bin/env, so its build, the test runner, root and the agents it forks all compile without registration. Measured: a realistic defn with four literals 5.14 -> 3.47 MB (-33%); a defn of n literals ~1.24 MB and ~172k ticks a literal registering, ~478 KB and ~52k not (-62%, -68%). Known: JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS is no input of a test's reach digest (bin/env fixes it), and Jolt's AOT cache keys a namespace without it, so a unit compiled with registration may be loaded without it and the reverse (harmless to Seon, which writes no image).", :changed #{"docs/forks.md#jolt" "docs/source.md#shared-build-caches" ":seon.config.build/jolt-compiler-options"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/source.md#shared-build-caches 60%: `bin/env` points Jolt's three compile caches at ``target/cache/`` of the main tree, found from git's common dir, so every worktree and generation (`bin/worktree --at`) shares them: `build` (`JOLT_BUILD_CACHE_DIR`, build units), `runtime` (`JOLT_RUNTIME_CACHE_DIR`) and `aot` (`JOLT_CACHE_DIR`, one artifact per namespace, keyed by its source, its requires' keys and what its compile read). No key holds a checkout's path: a unit is keyed by its text and the building runtime's fingerprint, and an AOT artifact's `.res` sidecar names each file its compile read (a schema `seon.contract` slurps) and each directory it listed (`resources/seon/schemas`, the checkout root `seon.ref/roots` lists) relative to the project root, so a checkout hashes its own files (Jolt ``jolt/cache-records-relative``). Entries and sidecars are published by temp+rename, so concurrent builds are safe; `JOLT_CACHE_GENERATIONS` (4) runtimes are kept, one per Jolt pin in use. `bin/worktree` clones neither these caches nor a checkout's old `target/home/.jolt` copies of them. `bin/build` clears nothing on a schema change: an edited schema file misses its 52 dependents of `seon.contract`, a schema file added to the directory moves the same keys. One sidecar holds the last compile's names, so two checkouts whose reads differ in which files exist (a worktree session that added a schema) take turns: each misses those namespaces once after the other built (measured: 41 misses, +6 s), never loading the other's artifact. Measured: an unchanged `bin/build seon` 25 s, pass 1 1.1 s, 263/263 hits; a fresh generation worktree (`bin/worktree --at`, 2.0 s, `target/home` 82 MB where the clone was 1.3 GB) builds from the shared cache in 24 s, pass 1 1.3 s, 263/263 hits; a namespace edited and built in one worktree is a hit in the next (16 misses there, 0 here); `bin/cache-smoke` checks that every checkout names the same three directories. `bin/env` also exports Jolt's compile options, `JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS` `{:image-fn-forms false}`, which Jolt reads into `*compiler-options*`' root at start: Seon writes no state image, so its build, tests, root and agents compile without registering fn literals' sources for one (a third less allocation a compile; task jolt.backend/image-registration-is-opt-in). The AOT key does not include it, so an artifact compiled with registration loads into a process without it and the reverse: harmless