{:task "seon.cost/ticks-are-exact", :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store) under seon.source-test/a-fixed-change-costs-the-same-at-any-program-size: the transact row's ticks differed between runs, 406.8k / 425k / 479.6k at 10 / 100 / 1,000 in one bin/prove shard against 406.3k / 424.8k / 477.1k in another process, 0.5% at 1,000, enough to move a growth fit by 0.02. docs/cost.md calls ticks exact. Reproduction (scratch script, seon.profile/measure! of d/db-with on seon.source-test/transact-of 10, 40 samples per process): within a process the count is exact, 406,355-406,362 per sample. The first call reads 409,285 (warming, which measure's warm call takes). About one sample in 40 reads 1,000 more (Chez's timer quantum, docs/cost.md). Holding 300 MB of live data first changes nothing (no collection ran during any sample). Two causes. (1) Fixed: seon.cost/measure repeated a call until 20 ms of CPU time or 25 calls, a clock deciding how many samples a counted dimension's minimum is taken over: a fast call took 25 and a slow or loaded one 3. It now takes seon.cost/repeats (3) after its warm call, whatever a call costs (seon.cost-test/a-measure-takes-a-count-of-samples-never-a-time: 26 calls before, 4 after). (2) Not yet found: the shard's process counts 0.1-0.5% more ticks for the same call than a fresh one. It is not the heap, and not a collection. It is what ran earlier in that process, e.g. protocol-dispatch caches (Jolt's PIC and resolve-memo, invalidated by every registration), Datahike's global caches, or the store's id counters giving other entity ids and so other index paths. Next: measure the row in one fresh process after requiring and running each other test namespace of its shard, bisect to the one that moves the count, and fix at that state's owner, so the same call counts the same in any process. Measured 2026-10-23 in ~/src/seon-next (scratch scripts; jolt -A:test run, quiet machine). (a) The tick read is exact: one loop measured after burning 0-1,200 ticks inside an armed measure (the timer's 1,000-tick chunk phase walked) read 20,007 at all 33 phases; a pure 130k-tick loop read 130,003 40 of 40; an allocating loop 1,359,548 with +11..+20 on 4 of 40 (the collection handler, as documented). (b) Not process state: loading and running six other test namespaces (print, render, cost, proofs, db, program) moved the transact row by under 20 ticks; 300 MB of live heap moved nothing; a collection forced before every call changed nothing (so not eq-hashtables rehashed after a collection). (c) d/db-with on the same arguments (seon.source-test/transact-of 10) counts differently from call to call within one process: about +-50 ticks around 409,100, and a whole +1,000 on 15-20% of calls (60% with the tx instant pinned by binding datahike.db.transaction/next-tx-instant, which removed the slow drift but not the spread). So the measured call is not the same input twice. The next suspect: each transaction takes fresh ids from the store's id source (datahike.ids, issued once across every branch) and a new commit id, so its values, their hashes and the index paths they take differ per call. Next: pin the id source (and the commit id) around one call, and see whether the count becomes exact. If it does, a cost proof over a transaction measures with its ids pinned, or seon.cost/measure states that the call consumes a shared source. The +1,000 granularity is itself a lead: a whole chunk suggests an event at a chunk boundary inside a region with interrupts disabled (Datahike's writer locks), where Chez re-arms the trap without charging the count. Second time-box (2026-10-23): ids and commit id are not involved, since d/db-with is a speculation that takes no ids (its tx is the db's max-tx + 1, its entities max-eid + 1, no commit). Pinned instant again: the variation is all inside datahike.db.transaction/transact-tx-data (floor 407,040, spread 1,082, +1,000 on 17 of 30 calls), most of it apply-db-op (339k of 411k), and the +1,000s vanish when that path is instrumented with tick reads (0 of 30). Interrupt-disabled regions alone do not cause them: loops of 3,000 tick reads, locking or atom swaps each count identically 30 of 30. Next suspect: the call allocates about 1.3 MB, and on the calls whose allocation crosses the collect trip, Chez raises a collect request whose handler (Jolt's GC policy, Scheme, on this thread) runs and is charged. When the policy collects nothing, no collection is counted and seon.cost's allowance takes nothing off. Whether a call crosses the trip depends on what was allocated before it, which is what makes it look random, and what a shard changes. Next: count and time the collect-request handler's runs during a measure (wrap Chez's collect-request-handler through jolt.scheme), and see whether every +1,000 call holds one. If so, the fix is in Jolt's tick count (a handler's ticks off the measured thread's count, as a collection's are) or in seon.cost/once (handler runs counted like collections). Third look (2026-10-23): the collect-request handler is not it. Over 40 calls of the pinned transaction, Chez's (collections) rose by 1, in one call that read +1,083, while 23 other calls read +1,041 to +1,048 with no collection, and a wrapper on the installed collect-request-handler counted 0 runs. So the jump is one 1,000-tick timer chunk charged to some calls and not others, with no collection and no ids. The rest of the spread is 0-46 ticks. Next suspect: the chunk boundaries and Chez's event entry (s/library.ss event: with interrupts disabled at a chunk's end it re-arms default-timer-ticks without charging timer-ticks, and an event raised by another thread runs at the next boundary). The count then depends on where chunk ends fall relative to the call's interrupt-disabled regions, or on another thread's events. Jolt's own tick reads add such regions: a loop of 3,000 tick reads counts 15,003 against a plain 30,000-iteration loop's 30,003, its reads' own ticks uncharged. Next test: count the event entries during one call and how many find interrupts disabled (a Chez-level counter: a patched event entry, or timer-interrupt-handler where the arm allows), against the jump. The fix then belongs to Jolt's count (fibers.ss jolt-set-timer!, jolt-thread-ticks*), charging a chunk only for the ticks the trap actually ran.", :changed #{"seon.cost/repeats" "docs/cost.md#what-is-measured" "seon.cost/measure"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#what-is-measured 79%: A proof measures the function under test, on the thread that calls it, and nothing else in the process (task `seon.cost/a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread`):" "docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged 54%: Ticks allow one timer chunk. A measure of a call's ticks may read one 1,000-tick chunk more than another measure of the same call on the same input, plus about 50, whatever the load (task `seon.cost/ticks-are-exact`: 40 calls of one pinned transaction read 407,097 + 0..46 on 17 and + 1,041..1,048 on 23, no collection in any; the chunk's cause in Chez is open there). ```seon.cost/judge``` takes `:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum` (1,050) off a size's ticks before judging it over its estimate, and against the growth before fitting it (the smallest size's up, the others' down), so one stray chunk is never a red while a real regression still is." "docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 20%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract 19%: A function's contract may carry its cost bound beside its schema: `:seon.cost/bound`, a plain function of the same arguments answering what one call may cost, `{:ticks :bytes :calls}` (any of them) and `:out`, the measure of its answer that a caller's bound passes on to the next callee (```seon.cost/bound``` reads it)." "docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used 17%: An agent's task names its cost proofs as goals ([agent](agent.md)); `bin/test` runs them with every other test; a proof's `:ops` are priced in ticks, bytes and time from the unit table `seon.cost/units`, which `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` measures again and holds to 1.25x."], :functions ["seon.cost/tick-allowance 18%: Ticks a measure allows for each collection that ran during it: a collection or signal raised from another thread runs its handler on the measured thread, and those ticks count as the call's (+11 to +27 per forced event, measured by dry-run: docs/cost.md); a quiet run is exact." "seon.cost/exact 8%: The dimensions a verdict is decided on: counted, so the same code on the same input measures the same on any machine at any load, and a green is a function of its reach digest (AGENTS.md section 5, results reused by input digest)." "seon.profile/measure! 4%: f called and measured, its value answered: the engine ticks it ran (the work it did, the exact measure: jolt.host/ticks-of arms the tick timer, and the count is read in a finally inside it, so a call that throws is counted), the bytes this thread allocated, its wall ns (now-ns) and its thread's CPU ns (cpu-ns), and the contracted calls of the epoch it opens (begin!)." "seon.cost/units 4%: Measured costs of unit operations on this runtime (release seon binary, M5 Max, load 5, at 10k units; source mode is within 10% except a plain call, 7 ns; the last five rows were measured in source mode after P2 found :str/append mislabeled: the 308 B per char it showed was the String's seq, not the StringBuilder; :fn/armed-call and the pull rows re-measured on Jolt 00a3b321, J3's try/catch: 990 -> 354 B per armed call), per unit: estimates price their :ops with these." "seon.cost/baseline 4%: What measuring a call that does nothing costs: the clock, tick and allocation reads themselves."], :schemas [[:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "37%"] [:seon.cost/dims "32%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled "30%"] [:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "19%"] [:seon.cost/unsettled "12%"]]}}
