{:task "seon.test-store/a-kept-store-is-keyed-by-its-schema", :plan "Found 2026-10-23 by root: the page merge changed three attributes to refs, and every checkout's kept target/program-store and target/runs/test-store refused to open (seon.db/open!: :seon.db/types-differ, another value type than the schema declares). bin/test failed at its plan, and process tests at root start. Both are regenerable output, made again from their inputs, never records. Fix: seon.db/schema-digest names the schema; seon.test-store/kept-store! answers base-<digest> and removes a store at base of another schema (base itself, or base-<8 hex digits>), sparing a runs directory beside it. The test runner's program store and the process store (seon.test-store/process-store, made by the plan's seon.test-store/make-process-store!, which replaces bin/test's seed step) are named by it. A changed schema makes a new store; nothing is migrated or refused.", :changed #{"seon.db/schema-digest" "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 66%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check. The merge's proof is `bin/prove` on the merged tree, then `bin/check smoke`; `bin/laws` (every law's sites) and `health` ([health](health.md)) run inside it as steps keyed by the whole tree. One rule selects for everyone: a key runs exactly when no green result is stored at its reach digest (```seon.proofs/plan```), in the one results directory every checkout shares (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`), so an unchanged tree runs nothing and a result proven in any checkout is reused in every other." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 11%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 9%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/checks.md#modes 8%: A step is a mode of `target/seon` (except `laws`, which is `bin/laws`, `lmdb-kill` and `lmdb-sem`, native programs on LMDB envs of their own, and `forks`, `bin/forks`: the forks' own suites on Jolt, [forks](forks.md#own-suites)), its body over one harness that boots root, runs it, and ends on the one verdict line `NAME OK` or `NAME FAILED` with its exit status (`seon.check/run-check`, [machine](machine.md#builds-and-checks))." "docs/storage.md#plain-values 5%: A value that is plain data (a map, vector, set or list of strings, numbers, keywords, symbols, uuids and instants) is stored as data: its attribute is ``:db.type/data`` in [our Datahike](forks.md), and the store's serializer (jfasl) writes it with no text in between."], :functions [["seon.db/install-missing! 4%: Transact the attributes of `schema` the db under conn lacks, or holds without their declared :db/contentId, and answer the idents it added: first :seon.tx/runtime alone when it is missing (the one transaction that cannot name its runtime, since it installs the attribute), then the rest in one transaction carrying tx-meta (an attribute it holds as another value type is refused, :seon.db/types-differ: a value type is fixed once installed; one it holds without its declared content id or index gains it): a kept store seeded before an attribute existed gains it when a root opens it, and a branch cut before one existed when its agent resumes it (seon.agent's resume: a branch keeps the schema it was cut with; Datahike accepts new attribute definitions at any time)." [:seon.db/types-differ :seon.error/fix :seon.error/message :seon.tx/branch :seon.tx/runtime]] ["seon.agent/test-plan 3%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.db/open! 2%: Root's main on the durable store at path." [:seon.db/branch :seon.db/open]] ["seon.root/import-source! 2%: Main's program follows Seon's own source (seon.source): root's dirs read through the :source effect (first brought to the main checkout's branch, seon.fx/read-source: fast-forwarded when behind, a recorded gap when it holds commits main lacks; their files, the tree's HEAD commit and its reference-code pins, and the tree's inputs with no program facts, seon.fx/inputs)." [:form/id :ns/file :schema/file :seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/source]] ["seon.db/make! 2%: A store made whole at path, or none (task seon.db/a-store-is-made-whole-or-not-at-all): built in a sibling directory, path.making-<uuid> (created, seeded with about n datoms, synced, its connection released so its env closes), then renamed to path, which is atomic on one filesystem." [:seon.db/store-unplaced :seon.error/fix :seon.error/message]]], :schemas [[:seon.db/types-differ "27%"] [:seon.config.source/gate-checkout "7%"] [:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes "6%"] [:seon.config.proofs/program-store "5%"] [:seon.task/state "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.step/an-errors-message-is-read-one-way", :plan "Found by the one-offs survey (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md; owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). Item 4. What exists: (or (ex-message e) (str (type e))) written out 15 times (seon.fx eight times, seon.docs, seon.program, seon.agent, seon.server, seon.model, my.schema) beside seon.step/error-msg, which is that function and has 5 users. Correct: every site calls step/error-msg (or the error map's owner, where one is built). A law over the compiler's facts: no src form but error-msg holds that shape (read as data).", :changed #{"seon.fx/worker-proc" "seon.fx/write-file" "seon.fx/resolve-deps" "seon.server/respond" "seon.agent/error-map" "seon.fx/wiki-history" "my.schema/judged" "seon.program/load-form!" "seon.docs/baseline-reads" "seon.fx/git-commit" "seon.model/http-post" "seon.fx/read-source"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics 32%: An exception from an evaluated form is the `:seon.agent/eval-error` map (`seon.agent/error-map`). Its ex-data is data on that map: every `:jolt.error/` and `:clojure.error/` key stays a key (Jolt's `:jolt.error/line`, `/column`, `/symbol`, `/ns`, `/kind`, `/type`, `/suggestions`, and `:clojure.error/*`; the schema is in `seon.problems.edn`), any other ex-data, qualified or not, is `:data`, a map. Jolt's \"did you mean\" suggestions are `:seon.error/fix`, and an unresolved symbol's message is built from `:jolt.error/symbol` as Jolt's nREPL words it (`jolt.nrepl/err->msg`), so the suggestions are not in it twice. `(prinltn 1)` reads `Unable to resolve symbol: prinltn in this context`, `fix: did you mean print, printf, println`, then the type, the operation and the remaining keys (line, column). `seon.repl/located` adds where, in the form's own terms (what `(source v)` prints, 1-based): `:seon.error/at {:line :column}`, and for a runtime error the fn it was in as `:seon.error/in`. Jolt's own positions are the request's text's, so a compile error is shifted by its form's start and a runtime error by its var's `:line`; the request-relative `:jolt.error/` and `:clojure.error/` line and column stay in the data, and out of the text only when `:seon.error/at` is stated. A runtime error reads `at line 3 of ns/f`, a compile error `at line 3:6` (a form with no recorded position keeps the request's, `:jolt.error/line`); proof `seon.repl-test/an-eval-error-says-where-in-its-forms-own-text`. An item's `:err-text` is a second error render, not the first: `(str err (nrepl/err->msg ..))`, the form's `*err*` output followed by Jolt's text with the request-relative position. It stays only because `repl_test` reads it; task `seon.repl/err-text-is-the-err-stream` splits the stream out and deletes it. The one render prints each fact once (`seon.render.ui/error-hidden` names the keys its own lines already carry, and the request-relative position only once the form's own is stated); proofs `seon.repl-test/an-eval-errors-data-is-printed-once` and `an-ordinary-exception-is-its-error-map`." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 15%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/render.md#one-printer 9%: There is one value printer, `seon.print`, and nothing is cut before it." "docs/resolution.md#sanctioned 8%: The sites that resolve a name held in data to a var by design, each with its reason." "docs/failure-points.md#entrant-boundaries-a-defect-becomes-a-recorded-error-map 7%: The boundaries where a defect in a request becomes a recorded error map instead of ending its process."], :functions [["seon.judge/form-facts 5%: What the compiler says each form of a reply's eval code does, read and analyzed in namespace nsym as loading would (seon.source/analyze-form: its ns form runs, so call this only in a disposable process, first-move's agent), with *read-eval* off: per form its :defs (the vars it defines) and :refs (the vars it references, its quoted data's and its macros' included), or :error, :seon.judge/uncompiled, for a form that does not compile (an unresolved name) or code that does not read: the model's text is the outside world (docs/failure-points.md)." [:seon.judge/uncompiled]] ["seon.docs/baseline-reads 4%: The baseline of the sections of plan (baseline-plan) from the history answer (seon.fx/wiki-history's value {:sections :files :errors}): {address {:read {name form-id} :unread reason}}." [:form/ns :form/src :ns/name :var/form :var/name]] ["seon.agent/error-map 4%: An exception caught at one of the agent's entrant boundaries (operation: the REPL's seon.repl/eval-code by default) as the one error map: its ex-data when that already is one (a contract's error names its operation, member and problems), else the :seon.agent/eval-error variant, with the exception's class, its ex-data's diagnostic keys (Jolt's :jolt.error/* : line, column, symbol, ns, kind, suggestions; and :clojure.error/*) as keys, the suggestions as the fix, any other ex-data as :data, and its cause chain (each cause's class and message)." [:seon.agent/eval-error :seon.error/base :seon.error/fix]] ["seon.render.proc/view-pass 4%: View-id rendered (pass-view), or, when its render throws, its page is that error: the edge of a failure point (docs/failure-points.md: a render is code, and agents write renders), so one view's defect stays that view's and every other view of the pass renders." [:seon.error/in :seon.error/message :seon.render/view-defect]] ["seon.fx/read-source 4%: The text of every .clj and .edn file under root's dirs (relative paths, e.g. src/seon/fx.clj, resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn), sorted by path, the commit root's HEAD names and the revision it pins each reference-code submodule at (its gitlink), both absent when root is no git repository (git answers none)." [:seon.fx/io-error]]], :schemas [[:seon.root/no-source-root "5%"] [:seon.root/no-source-text "4%"] [:seon.server/defect "3%"] [:seon.problems/class "3%"] [:seon.error/base "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.step/an-edge-asks-one-way", :plan "Found by the one-offs survey (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 8; owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). What existed: an edge's request to a proc written three times: seon.root/call! (a promise put with the request, a closed :ext answered root stopped), seon.web's ask! (the same, but a closed channel left its HTTP thread waiting forever) and seon.root/stop!'s :shutdown (a closed :ext waited out both of stop!'s bounds, about 18 s, on a promise nothing would deliver). Correct: seon.step/ask-edge, beside the asker's side every proc shares (pend, ask, answered), puts the request with a fresh promise and answers a closed channel at once with the caller's closed value; the three call it. A law: no src form outside seon.step puts a request carrying :p on a channel (read as data from the compiler's facts).", :changed #{"seon.root/call!" "seon.web/ask!" "seon.root/stop!" "seon.step/ask-edge" "docs/flow.md#idioms-for-seon"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/flow.md#idioms-for-seon 71%: How Seon meets each need (a dependency, a backlog, a delayed retry, quiet) on Flow." "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data 18%: The failure points met without a catch, where the edge answers with data." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 10%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/config.md#constants 4%: Every limit, bound or default is a config key (AGENTS §5)." "docs/flow.md#the-proc-contract-reference-codejoltstdlibclojurecoreasyncflowclj-process-reference-codejoltstdlibclojurecoreasyncflowimplclj-proc 3%: When Flow calls each of a proc's four functions and what it may return: describe once, init at start, transition on a status change."], :functions ["seon.web/ask! 7%: m asked of the web proc on its :http in-port (seon.step/ask-edge), and its one answer waited for: the edge's blocking read; the proc never waits." ["seon.root/call! 5%: Put m (with a fresh reply promise) on the api; a promise." [:seon.root/request :seon.root/sys]] ["seon.root/await-db 4%: Main's first db value (now or after a later commit) for which (pred db) holds: a watch on main's connection runs pred once per commit." [:seon.root/no-agent :seon.root/quiet-before-true]] ["seon.root/stop! 4%: Stop root by letting the flow drain: the api closes (later calls answer \"root stopped\"); every agent is sent :stop and exits after the requests before it (SIGKILL past seon.agent-proc/stop-bound-ms); the writer's in-port closes, it commits and flushes what it took; the fork server is killed and reaped; then flow/stop (every proc exited) and the reader." [:seon.root/id :seon.root/stopped :seon.root/sys]] ["seon.root/api-proc 3%: The api." [:seon.config.root/api-batch :seon.config.root/api-writes :seon.step/step-fn]]], :schemas [[:seon.root/quiet-before-true "6%"] [:seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd "6%"] [:seon.task/state "6%"] [:seon.root/stopped "5%"] [:seon.reconcile/action "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.config/a-bound-is-a-config-key", :plan "AGENTS section 5, only documented config: every limit, bound or default is a key in resources/seon/config/default.edn with its unit, provenance and what happens at the bound. Found by the one-offs survey (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 6), and no law caught them: seon.proofs/max-assertions 8, seon.root/tail-bytes 2048, seon.print.emit/nest-depth 6 and root's view windows (take-last 20, twice) were literals in src. Correct: they are :seon.config.proofs/max-assertions, :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes, :seon.config.render/nest-depth and :seon.config.root/view-window (the windows through seon.lib/push-window); the numbers left in src are named in docs/config.md#constants with their reasons (clocks their tasks delete, the cost judge's tolerances its own task derives, a digest's width), and a law reads src's forms from the compiler's facts and is red on a top-level def of a number or a literal count over 2 given to take, take-last, drop or drop-last that the page does not name, or on a name there whose site is gone. A foreign interface's upper-case constant is not a bound. Deletes the four literals.", :changed #{"seon.root/set-agent" ":seon.config.proofs/max-assertions" "seon.root/back-op" ":seon.config.render/nest-depth" ":seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes" "docs/config.md#constants" "seon.print.emit/structural-kinds" "seon.proofs/failures" ":seon.config.root/view-window" "seon.render.ai/problem-lines" "seon.print.emit/nest-depth" "seon.root/stderr-tail"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#cost-proofs 9%: **Cost is work done (ticks, bytes, calls), never wall or CPU time** (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I like ticks vs wall clock or anything that is load dependent." "docs/clocks.md#clocks 9%: Time is measured and recorded, and decides only at the owner's three exceptions; `bin/laws clocks` and `bin/laws measures` name every place in `src/` that reads or waits on a clock." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 6%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/config.md#config 5%: Every limit, bound and default of the platform is one key in one aero file, documented beside its value, validated by its schema, and read through one resolver." "docs/resolution.md#resolution 5%: A name resolves at run time, or a Var is defined outside its definition form, only where this page names the function and its reason; `bin/laws resolution` lists every such site in `src/`."], :functions ["seon.agent-proc/stop-bound-ms 6%: How long an agent may take to exit after :stop before it is SIGKILLed." "seon.cost/tick-allowance 5%: Ticks a measure allows for each collection that ran during it: a collection or signal raised from another thread runs its handler on the measured thread, and those ticks count as the call's (+11 to +27 per forced event, measured by dry-run: docs/cost.md); a quiet run is exact." "seon.cost/growth-share 5%: Growth is fitted on increments over the smallest size only when the largest increment is at least this share of the smallest size's cost: below it, the measure's own variance moves the increments more than the growth does." "seon.lib/PAGE 5%" ["seon.root/back-op 5%: A message from an agent proc, a fork-server or writer event, or Flow's error channel." [:seon.config.root/view-window :seon.error/message :seon.root/api-state :seon.root/proc-defect :seon.task/cause :seon.task/id]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes "39%"] [:seon.config.root/view-window "22%"] [:seon.config.proofs/max-assertions "18%"] [:seon.config/config "8%"] [:seon.config.render/big-alloc "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.lib/real-mb-reads-what-macos-charges", :plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23). What existed: bin/check scale's root gauge, seon.lib/real-mb, read 398-404 MB for a root whose phys_footprint was 1,460 MB with the embedding model open, and 856 MB on Seon's own program without it (jolt/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding). Cause, measured: real-mb summed the private and shared-now-private resident pages of the process's VM regions, and a fork turns the parent's pages copy-on-write, so they left the sum: a parent holding a 730 MB block read 76 MB after forking one child (its phys_footprint stayed 730 MB); root forks the fork server, so it read about a quarter of what it holds. The note that macOS's footprint overstates a forked child does not hold for the task's own ledger: a child read 3 MB at the fork and 43 MB after writing 30 MB of the shared block. Fixed: real-mb is the task's phys_footprint (task_info TASK_VM_INFO: private dirty pages, compressed ones included, what Activity Monitor and jetsam charge), for root, the fork server and each agent; native/probe.c answers the ledger only, and its region walk (~1.5 ms a read) is deleted. bin/check scale's need reads it.", :changed #{"docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "docs/machine.md#the-target-a-16-gb-macbook-m1" "seon.lib/real-mb" "seon.agent/mem-mb" "docs/cost.md#how-we-measure" "seon.lib/mp-probe" ":seon.lib/probe"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 77%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/machine.md#the-target-a-16-gb-macbook-m1 57%: Seon is built to run well on a 16 GB MacBook M1: it stays within `:seon.config.machine/memory-mb` (4096: root, the fork server and the default number of agents), and `bin/check scale` fails past it. A cost that grows with something other than the work (a probe walking a 64 GiB address-space reservation took 100 agents from 0.6 s to 18 s) is found by a cost proof sized by what it walks ([storage](storage.md#the-map-is-address-space)). A process's memory is what macOS charges it, its `phys_footprint` (`seon.lib/real-mb`): a forked child is charged what it writes, and the parent keeps its own charge ([cost](cost.md#how-we-measure)). Like every bound, the key lives in `resources/seon/config/default.edn` ([config](config.md))." "docs/vector.md#the-model 50%: EmbeddingGemma-2 (768 dims, Matryoshka, mean-pooled, normalized) runs in process through llama.cpp: `native/embed.c` puts its encoder behind a C ABI of pointers and ints (open, embed a batch of texts given as one UTF-8 buffer and offsets, close), and `bin/build embed` builds llama.cpp v0.7.0 from `reference-code/llama.cpp` (static, Metal, no OpenMP) with it into `target/lib/libseon-embed.dylib`. The model file is ggml-org's Q8_0 GGUF, fetched once by `bin/build` into the main tree's `target/cache/models/`, named by its sha256 and checked against it (`bin/env`'s `SEON_EMBED_MODEL`); its name is the model's id on every vector (`seon.vector/model-id`), and `bin/env` is the one place the pin is written (`:seon.config.vector/model` reads it), so a model change makes every vector due again. Nothing is installed outside `target/`; cmake is the one added prerequisite." "docs/storage.md#the-map-is-address-space 16%: LMDB's map (`:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes`, 64 GiB) is address space only: an open or a fork costs the same at 1 or 64 GiB, and the file grows only as it is used." "docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads 14%: Three rules of the OS and Chez that no code may \"fix\" by adding a thread or a check:"], :functions [["seon.lib/mp-probe 5%: This process's memory as macOS's ledger holds it (native/probe.c, task_info TASK_VM_INFO): {:phys-fp :internal :compressed :resident} in MB." [:seon.error/message :seon.error/type :seon.lib/probe]] ["seon.agent/mem-mb 5%: This process's memory as macOS charges it, read now (lib/real-mb: one task_info call): on the events that report the agent's state (its hello, each commit, a :tests reply, :stats), never per evaluation and never kept for a time." [:seon.lib/mb]] ["seon.fork-server/server-step 4%: The fork server's step-fn." [:seon.config.root/system-path :seon.step/step-fn]] ["seon.agent/init! 2%: Make this process the agent: its socket (fd), its namespace, its state map, its interrupt handlers." [:seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable :seon.lib/fd]] ["seon.root/import-source! 2%: Main's program follows Seon's own source (seon.source): root's dirs read through the :source effect (first brought to the main checkout's branch, seon.fx/read-source: fast-forwarded when behind, a recorded gap when it holds commits main lacks; their files, the tree's HEAD commit and its reference-code pins, and the tree's inputs with no program facts, seon.fx/inputs)." [:form/id :ns/file :schema/file :seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/source]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.check/pages-ms "5%"] [:seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd "4%"] [:seon.lib/probe "4%"] [:seon.task/state "3%"] [:seon.fork-server/forked "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.render/page", :plan "Every page is the walk of one entity (seon.render/walk) through its :html face (owner, 2026-10-23: \"no separate run view it's part of the agents view\"; \"root is also an agents view, it just has different focus since it should be linked up with all the agents it's running on the system, the system health, etc\"). What exists: seon.render.proc/zoom-value assembles an agent's window by hand from root's view, main's run and the branch, joined by strings (turn-rows, eval-records, a tile list, a run-or-agent branch, a :turn-ctx cache), the /run/{id} route beside /agent/{id}, and root's page a second producer (seon.render.ui/system-page, agent-card and the grid). Build: a location is an entity (/agent/{id} resolves to the agent's eid in root's boot, / to root's); the page is the walk: the entity as the main pane (:block), its units the column (:tile) in their declared order, each a link that moves the eid; a unit is rendered again when a commit changes its entity (the render proc's events), never polled. The owner's window is the same evaluations the model's context shows (seon.context/evaluations), through the :html face: one list, newest at the top, each evaluation rendered once (its thought, its form, its result by the result's own render, its cost), a model turn grouping its evaluations under its header; no second output (no transcript beside a column of the same results), and no special place for now: it is the newest evaluation (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I'm wondering if we don't just limit it to now and instead it's every eval that renders to html sorted by newest at the top\"; \"no I don't want separate outputs I want responsive html and css so why not tailwind?\"). Layout is responsive CSS: a render tags its root with how it is shown (:as) and Tailwind's classes lay it out at each width; the content a render includes is its own choice by :as (an :inline value is one line, for the :ai face too). Tailwind as v1 built it, ported to Jolt's build (owner, 2026-10-23: \"look at the ~/src/seon I think we had something for tailwind generation? but build it for the jolt build process so it's simple and we can just update the css and it regenerrates without a lot of fuss\"): v1's ~/src/seon/bin/css is a two-argument transform, Tailwind v4 CSS-first (every directive, token and source glob in resources/public/css/input.css, no config file), measured 171-187 ms, a build product and never a runtime component (v1 deleted its Integrant watcher). Keep v1's lessons: `@import \"tailwindcss\" source(none)` with explicit `@source` globs over src/**/*.clj (auto-detection walked the reference-code tree: 42 s, against ~1 s), and a safelist of the utility vocabulary agents may use, since a render an agent defines at its REPL is never in a scanned file. On Jolt: Tailwind's standalone v4 binary in place of bun and node_modules, pinned by version and checksum, fetched into target/ and run as a step of bin/build (and so of the :build effect after merges), its output reused by the digest of input.css and the scanned sources (AGENTS §5): edit input.css or a render's classes, build, and the stylesheet is regenerated; unchanged inputs regenerate nothing. The hand-written app.css goes. The chat input (evaluate, message, interrupt, release) stays at the top, where the newest is. Only what is on screen is rendered (owner, 2026-10-23: \"We can dynamically load the tiles and not show the ones offscreen right?\"): the page carries the newest units and a placeholder saying how many older there are; when it scrolls into view (Datastar's data-on-intersect) the render proc renders the next ones, so the server works only for what is looked at, by that event, never a poll. Deletes zoom-value, turn-rows, eval-records' joins, the /run route, system-page's hand-built sections, agent-card and the grid (one :tile render of an agent replaces both), the tile list and :turn-ctx. A browser subagent compares each page against the facts (notes/browser-tester.md). The old window's last browser round (2026-10-23, agent a5) found what the new page must not repeat: a count of evaluations the list does not show (every evaluation is a row, or the count is not shown); a name shortened without saying so (the branch's :main/ prefix); a tile that says 0 without saying nothing has run; numbers without their unit or label, and a warning without its bound.", :changed #{":seon.render/page-head" "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "seon.render.ui/more" "docs/dashboard.md#the-dashboard" ":seon.message/fact" "seon.render.proc/ask-branches" "seon.render.ui/view-param" ":seon.render/no-location" ":seon.render/parts" ":seon.root/root-fact" ":seon.cluster.eval/record" "seon.render.ui/cost-line" "docs/render.md#the-page" "seon.render.ui/run-row" "seon.render.proc/page-value" "docs/dashboard.md#the-render-proc" "seon.render.ui/thought" "seon.render.ui/agent-input" ":seon.config.render/line-budget" "docs/render.md#bounded-lists" "seon.render.proc/pass-view" ":seon.config.web/public-dir" ":seon.render/no-entity" "seon.render.ui/tile" "docs/render.md#the-walk" "seon.render/shown" "docs/render.md#the-page-is-units" "seon.render.proc/pass" ":seon.render/t" ":seon.render/system" ":seon.render/agent-input" "seon.render/merged" "seon.web/stylesheet" "seon.render.proc/on-event" "seon.render.ui/row" ":seon.render/eid" "seon.web/handler" "seon.web/http-in" "docs/render.md#cuts-in-a-display-namespace-that-are-not-cuts-of-output" ":seon.db/eval-fact" ":seon.render/root-input" "seon.render.ui/shown-as" ":seon.render/page-more" ":seon.agent/item" ":seon.db/agent-fact" ":seon.config.web/stylesheet" "seon.render.proc/main-info" "seon.render.proc/ready?" "seon.render.ui/event-detail" "seon.render.ui/shown-status" "seon.render.ui/evaluation" "seon.render.proc/answered" "seon.render.ui/rows" "seon.render.proc/step-units" ":seon.render/page-row" "seon.render.proc/render-proc" ":seon.render/as" "docs/server.m