{:task "seon.context/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding", :plan "Done (docs/vector.md): semantic search native to the store. EmbeddingGemma-2 runs in process in root through llama.cpp v0.7.0 (native/embed.c, `bin/build embed`; the Q8_0 GGUF fetched once by its sha256 into the main tree's target/cache), as the `:embed` effect kind: a batch kind whose worker opens the model on its first request and keeps it (`seon.fx/worker-proc`'s `:open`, `seon.fx/dispatch`'s `:batch`: what is queued, never a timer). `seon.vector/vectors-proc` embeds each form's, wiki section's and task plan's text that `seon.vector/due` lists, at main's commit notice, round by round, and writes the vectors onto those entities (f16 whole, an int8 128-dim coarse row: `seon.vector/pack`); a form is content-addressed, so it is embedded once. `seon.vector/near` is a Datalog function, [(seon.vector/near $ ?q 10 {:of :form/id}) [[?e ?score]]], over a two-level C search (native/vector.c: int8 coarse scan, f16 rerank of the shortlist). the context's found (deleted by seon.render/a-page-is-its-entitys-view, 4d: it put unrelated helpers' source in every run) fused the call graph with the forms nearest the task's plan by reciprocal rank; `my.agent/similar` asks it at the REPL. Replaces: v1's MLX HTTP server and its client (`seon.embed`), em1's prototype kernel; the call-graph-only `:found`. Measured: recall@10 of the default levels against exact search 0.998 on Seon's whole text (`bin/check vectors`); helpers recall@10 on 283 commit pairs: call graph 0.042, embeddings 0.495, fused 0.451; one text 5-15 ms, 37k tokens/s batched, whole text embedded in 17-19 s at boot; `near` 4.6 ms in Jolt, 0.08 ms in C. Open: a task-shaped helpers corpus (proofs as sources) to weigh the fusion; the datom copy in `near` past ~100k vectors.", :changed #{"docs/parity.md#the-agents-repl-my" "seon.vector/texts" "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "seon.schema.datahike/type->datahike" "seon.context/current-state" "seon.vector/vector-of" "seon.fx/charge" "seon.root/start!" "seon.fx/worker-proc" "docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded" "docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph" "docs/README.md#the-running-system" "seon.system/build-fx" "seon.fx/owner-proc" "seon.vector/document-prompt" ":seon.config.vector/shortlist" ":seon.config.vector/batch" "docs/decisions.md#embeddings-join-the-context-fused-small-and-measured" "seon.vector/due" "seon.vector/embed-texts" "docs/forks.md#jolt" ":seon.vector/model" "seon.vector/model-id" ":seon.vector/tokens" "seon.vector/unpack" "seon.vector/vectors-proc" ":seon.config.vector/model-threads" "seon.vector/tx-data" "seon.fx/dispatch" ":seon.config.vector/coarse-dims" "seon.vector/pack" "seon.vector/stored" ":seon.config.vector/fine-dims" "seon.proofs/check-steps" "seon.fx/admit" "seon.context/found" ":seon.config.vector/batch-tokens" ":seon.config.vector/batch-texts" "my.agent/similar" "seon.fx/reply!" "seon.fx/procs" "seon.vector/open" "docs/vector.md#vectors" "seon.vector/near" "seon.vector/query-prompt" ":seon.config.vector/texts" "docs/vector.md#the-pyramid" "seon.fx/spent?" ":seon.vector/fact" ":seon.vector/model-unopened" ":seon.vector/encode-failed" ":seon.vector/f16" "seon.vector/pack-dims" ":seon.config.vector/model" ":seon.vector/text-t" ":seon.config.vector/rrf-k" ":seon.config.vector/lib" "seon.vector/embed" "seon.context/rrf" "seon.fx/batch" "docs/machine.md#builds-and-checks" ":seon.config.render/found-near" ":seon.config.vector/threads" ":seon.config.vector/max-tokens" "seon.vector/round" "seon.fx/outcome" "docs/flow.md#idioms-for-seon" ":seon.vector/coarse" "docs/vector.md#the-model"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/vector.md#the-pyramid 78%: A Matryoshka vector's prefix is itself an embedding, so one stored vector serves two levels (`native/vector.c`, in libprobe: no threads, every process loads it). The coarse row is the first `:seon.config.vector/coarse-dims` dims renormalized and scaled to int8, with its scale (`seon.vector/pack`); the coarse level scans every row with integer dot products and keeps the best `:seon.config.vector/shortlist` in a heap; the fine level ranks those at f16 over `:seon.config.vector/fine-dims` dims, each prefix renormalized. A query option changes either (`{:shortlist n :fine-dims d}`); a shortlist of every vector is the exact search. `near` reads the coarse rows from the db value each call (no cache: a db value is its own index), copies them into one buffer, and reads only the shortlist's f16 rows." "docs/vector.md#the-model 67%: EmbeddingGemma-2 (768 dims, Matryoshka, mean-pooled, normalized) runs in process through llama.cpp: `native/embed.c` puts its encoder behind a C ABI of pointers and ints (open, embed a batch of texts given as one UTF-8 buffer and offsets, close), and `bin/build embed` builds llama.cpp v0.7.0 from `reference-code/llama.cpp` (static, Metal, no OpenMP) with it into `target/lib/libseon-embed.dylib`. The model file is ggml-org's Q8_0 GGUF, fetched once by `bin/build` into the main tree's `target/cache/models/`, named by its sha256 and checked against it (`bin/env`'s `SEON_EMBED_MODEL`); its name is the model's id on every vector (`seon.vector/model-id`), and `bin/env` is the one place the pin is written (`:seon.config.vector/model` reads it), so a model change makes every vector due again. Nothing is installed outside `target/`; cmake is the one added prerequisite." "docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded 65%: Each kind's text is its attribute's value with the model's prompt: a form's `:form/src`, a section's `:seon.section/text` and a schema entry's `:schema/form` as documents (`seon.vector/document-prompt`), a task's `:seon.task/plan` as what a search asks (`seon.vector/query-prompt`). A text over `:seon.config.vector/max-tokens` keeps its first tokens, and special-token text in it is read as text. A vector is five facts on the entity: `:seon.vector/f16` (the whole vector), `:seon.vector/coarse` (the [coarse level](#the-pyramid)), `:seon.vector/model`, `:seon.vector/text-t` (the transaction of the text it embeds) and `:seon.vector/tokens` (the text's whole token count: past the bound, what was left out)." "docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph 65%: The task's data shows `:similar` (```seon.context/similar```): the first `:seon.config.render/similar` program functions nearest the plan's vector, by an exact search (`near` with every vector its shortlist, at every dim), that the plan and proofs do not name, a line each. On Seon's own program (3,843 vectors) it costs about 9.7M ticks and 52 MB (40 ms) and 397 of a task's data's 1,749 tokens; ```seon.vector-cost-test/similar-ranks-every-form-once``` holds it. Step 6 measures how often agents read one; 0 turns it off. Fusing the call graph's helpers (the cited vars' and proofs' one-hop callees, tf-idf) with the forms nearest a task's plan by reciprocal rank (```seon.vector/rrf```, k `:seon.config.vector/rrf-k`) was measured below; embeddings alone did as well, so the task's data uses them alone, and the fusion stays for further exploration." "docs/vector.md#what-the-work-is-likely-to-need-as-an-eval 55%: The page's `[:related]` part (```seon.render.ui/related```), which the model reads with the rest of the page, and `(my.task/related)` at the REPL, show what the plan's work is likely to need beyond what the plan cites (```seon.context/related```):"], :functions [["seon.context/similar 11%: The program functions nearest task entity te's plan by meaning (its vector: seon.vector/near, exact: every form's vector at every dim, no coarse first pass), those in named left out, the first :seon.config.render/similar of them, each one line: its name and its doc's first sentence ({:nearest :more}, :more the forms that search further)." [:form/id :ns/file :ns/name :seon.config.render/similar :seon.task/model :var/doc :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.context/related 10%: What the work on task entity te's plan is likely to need beyond what the plan cites: the :seon.config.render/related wiki sections, program functions and schema keys nearest it, each kind ranked by the plan's references and by meaning fused by reciprocal rank (seon.vector/rrf): the graph from its citations (the cited vars' callers and callees, or, when it cites no defined var, those of the functions nearest it by meaning; the sections naming a cited var, the sections a cited section references or is referenced by, the schema keys the plan names and the cited vars' forms name, :form/keywords) and seon.vector/near on the plan's vector." [:form/id :form/keywords :form/refs :ns/file :ns/name :schema/key :seon.config.render/related-near :seon.config.render/related-rarity :seon.config.render/related-seeds-near :seon.ref/address :seon.ref/name :seon.ref/path :seon.section/address :seon.section/names :seon.section/references :seon.task/id :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.agent/embed-written! 7%: The texts this branch wrote since the last call (since its fork, at the first: after my.branch/merge-main!, main's texts merged in too, which carry no vector) and its task's plan, those whose vector is due for model (seon.vector/due over seon.vector/written), embedded by root's :embed effect in one request (the first n of them; 0 asks nothing) and their vectors written on the branch: what seon.vector/near reads here follows the agent's own writes, and the model stays in root." [:seon.config.vector/model :seon.config.vector/texts :seon.error/type :seon.task/id :seon.task/plan :seon.vector/encode-failed]] "seon.vector/round 5%: At main's db, the next round: the first :texts due (:seon.config.vector/texts; 0 asks nothing) go to the :embed effect." ["my.agent/related 5%: The wiki sections nearest x by meaning, a text (embedded as a search's query by root's :embed effect) or a var's definition, k of them (:seon.config.render/related unless given), each a line at summary depth with the forms that read one whole and search further (seon.context/related-view): how an agent refines, in its own words, what its page's related part showed." [:seon.config.render/related :seon.section/address]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.vector/lib "8%"] [:seon.config.vector/model "5%"] [:seon.config.vector/coarse-dims "5%"] [:seon.config.vector/texts "4%"] [:seon.config.vector/shortlist "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.profile/a-call-is-measured-one-way", :plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs (DECISIONS). What exists: two measurements of one call. seon.repl/wrap-cost measures each evaluated form (wall ns by seon.profile/now-ns, allocated bytes, engine ticks counted in a finally inside jolt.host/ticks-of so a form that throws is counted, the costliest functions); seon.cost/once measures a cost proof's call (CPU ns, allocated bytes, ticks as ticks-of answers them, contracted calls). Correct: one seon.profile/measure! runs a function and answers its value, and hands what it measured ({:ticks :bytes :wall-ns :cpu-ns :calls}) to a callback from a finally, so a call that throws is measured and its exception goes on; the REPL and the cost proof each read what they show from it. A law keeps it one: no src form but measure! reads the tick counter (jolt.host/ticks-of or ticks). Deletes both inline measurements.", :changed #{"seon.fx/base-commit" "seon.profile/measure!" "seon.repl/wrap-cost" "docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used" "seon.cost/once" "docs/clocks.md#time-measured-recorded-never-compared-to-decide"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used 73%: An agent's task names its cost proofs as goals ([agent](agent.md)); `bin/test` runs them with every other test; a proof's `:ops` are priced in ticks, bytes and time from the unit table `seon.cost/units`, which `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` measures again and holds to 1.25x. Clocks that order events are `seon.profile/now-ns` ([machine](machine.md#the-clock)); `cpu-ns` orders nothing ([clocks](clocks.md))." "docs/cost.md#what-is-measured 66%: A proof measures the function under test, on the thread that calls it, and nothing else in the process (task `seon.cost/a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread`):" "docs/machine.md#the-clock 17%: Seon has one clock: `seon.profile/now-ns`, the machine's monotonic CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW read by a Chez foreign procedure (9.5 ns a read, nothing allocated, 41 ns resolution), and `seon.profile/ms-since` for \"milliseconds since t0\"." "docs/clocks.md#time-measured-recorded-never-compared-to-decide 12%: Clock reads whose value is recorded or shown and never compared to decide anything." "docs/cost.md#cost-proofs 10%: **Cost is work done (ticks, bytes, calls), never wall or CPU time** (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I like ticks vs wall clock or anything that is load dependent."], :functions ["seon.profile/measure! 37%: f called and measured, its value answered: the engine ticks it ran (the work it did, the exact\n  measure: jolt.host/ticks-of arms the tick timer, and the count is read in a finally inside it,\n  so a call that throws is counted), the bytes this thread allocated, its wall ns (now-ns) and\n  its thread's CPU ns (cpu-ns), and the contracted calls of the epoch it opens (begin!). What it\n  measured goes to done, {:ticks :bytes :wall-ns :cpu-ns :calls}, from a finally: a call that\n  throws is measured too, and its exception goes on to the caller. The one measurement of a call:\n  the REPL's per-form cost (seon.repl/wrap-cost) and a cost proof's (seon.cost/measure) read it." "seon.cost/tick-allowance 9%: Ticks a measure allows for each collection that ran during it: a collection or signal raised from another thread runs its handler on the measured thread, and those ticks count as the call's (+11 to +27 per forced event, measured by dry-run: docs/cost.md); a quiet run is exact." ["seon.cost/measure 5%: f on args, measured at its steady state: calls until `:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled` calls after the one that allocated the fewest bytes so far allocate no fewer; each dimension's minimum over all of them (bytes are the steady state's; ticks and calls their least, which noise only adds to), less what measuring itself costs." [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples :seon.config.proofs/cost-settled :seon.cost/dims :seon.cost/unsettled]] "seon.repl/middleware 5%: Outermost first." "seon.cost/baseline 4%: What measuring a call that does nothing costs: the clock, tick and allocation reads themselves."], :schemas [[:seon.config.agent/form-top-fns "42%"] [:seon.cost/dims "32%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "12%"] [:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "10%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled "7%"]]}}
{:task "seon.contract/an-armed-call-costs-its-checks", :plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; malli jolt/total-arms-unguarded bf35c354, 26aeb44c). A contracted call cost 354 B over a plain one (seon.cost/units :fn/armed-call, [:=> [:cat :int] :int]). Measured apart: the profile count 0 B; each arm's check 176 B, the input's and the output's alike, whatever it validated: malli's -instrument wrapped each check in a try (our fork's 56394c54, so a throwing validator is reported as ::invalid-schema-at-call, never as the function's error), and a Jolt try/catch is a one-shot escape continuation and a pushed handler, ~160-176 B per entry, near its floor (scheme-adapter-runtime.ss, jolt-try-body). Fixed in malli, where the cost is made: whether an arm can throw is decided once at instrument time (m/-total?): every node a type that answers for any realized value (the built-ins, malli's own predicate schemas but pos? neg? zero?, which are registered bare and throw on a non-number, its comparators, function schemas when no ::function-checker is set, and the types options name in ::total-types), named references followed; a :ref (lazy), a :fn or a user's own simple schema keeps its try. A lazy seq an argument holds can still throw while a sequence check realizes it: that throw is the caller's, as when the function reads it. Seon names its predicate pack total (seon.contract/options). Measured: an armed call 352 -> 0 B (the unit table's row: 12.2 -> 10.2 ticks; a bare -instrument of both arms, without the count: 8 -> 6); a call of [:=> [:cat :map :int] [:map]] 512 -> 144 B; seon.source/import-tx of src and test warm 2,193 -> 1,990 MB (-9%), ticks unchanged. The unit table's :fn/armed-call row re-measured.", :changed #{"docs/forks.md#malli" "seon.cost/units" "seon.contract/options"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/forks.md#malli 100%: `reference-code/malli` pinned at `26aeb44c`, ``jolt/total-arms-unguarded``, on `0762fb6b` (``jolt/mg-seed``: mg/check takes its `:seed` and returns it on failure), on top of `5a830e14` (``jolt/main``, ``jolt/instrument-arity``)." "docs/forks.md#jolt 15%: `reference-code/jolt` pinned at `6e604ac8`, the tip of ``jolt/emit-string-literals``, on `eba8826a`, the tip of ``jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in``, on `2a6ae8f8`, the tip of ``jolt/arrays-compare``, on `86f49de8`, the tip of ``jolt/analysis-lookups``, on ``jolt/record-field-binds`` `0d39edc3`, on `e0351af8` (``jolt/global-ids-pin``: ``jolt/pin-2026-10-23`` `f05de7db` with ``jolt/hashset-bulk`` `ce384f07` merged in)." "docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used 13%: An agent's task names its cost proofs as goals ([agent](agent.md)); `bin/test` runs them with every other test; a proof's `:ops` are priced in ticks, bytes and time from the unit table `seon.cost/units`, which `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` measures again and holds to 1.25x." "docs/cost.md#what-is-measured 12%: A proof measures the function under test, on the thread that calls it, and nothing else in the process (task `seon.cost/a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread`):" "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 11%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)."], :functions ["seon.contract/wrap 5%: f instrumented by the compiled function schema s, errors named fn-sym, and counted under fn-sym (seon.profile: every call counted, one in 64 timed, the check included), except seon.profile's own functions." ["seon.cost/run 3%: Estimate e measured: its function on its input at each size (sizes, under :seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n), ascending, or on each generated case (`cases`) against its contract's bound on that case's arguments (`bound`, kept on the row as :args), stopping after the first point over its estimate in an exact dimension." [:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n :seon.cost/estimate :seon.cost/row :seon.cost/too-few-sizes :seon.cost/verdict]] ["seon.proofs/test-vars 3%: clojure.test's test-vars for vars of namespace n (Jolt's: its :once fixtures around them, each var in its :each fixtures), each test with a budget of its own: ticks engine ticks of work (nil: unbounded), inside its :each fixtures, so their teardown runs whatever the test did." [:seon.error/type :seon.test/ticks-spent]] "seon.profile/measure! 2%: f called and measured, its value answered: the engine ticks it ran (the work it did, the exact measure: jolt.host/ticks-of arms the tick timer, and the count is read in a finally inside it, so a call that throws is counted), the bytes this thread allocated, its wall ns (now-ns) and its thread's CPU ns (cpu-ns), and the contracted calls of the epoch it opens (begin!)." "seon.cost/exact 2%: The dimensions a verdict is decided on: counted, so the same code on the same input measures the same on any machine at any load, and a green is a function of its reach digest (AGENTS.md section 5, results reused by input digest)."], :schemas [[:seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples "5%"] [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "3%"] [:seon.test/ticks-spent "3%"] [:seon.instrument/contract-error "3%"] [:seon.profile/fn-cost "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.cost/a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper", :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store, under seon.program-cost-test/a-change-re-folds-only-what-reaches-it): seon.cost/judge fits a dimension's growth on measured less the estimate's :fixed. When a change makes the work cheaper, the fixed part falls under the stated :fixed, and the smallest size's remainder shrinks toward zero, so the fitted exponent jumps. Jolt 0d39edc3 took reach-update from 500k to 427k ticks at 100 and from 1.24M to 1.07M at 1,000, under the estimate at both, and the fit on (427k - 420k, 1.07M - 420k) read n^1.96: red for a speedup. Re-deriving each proof's :fixed after every speedup is churn. Correct, in judge: growth is judged so that a uniform speedup can never read as steeper. Done (branch jolt/a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper, docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged): with three sizes or more, each size's increment over the smallest is fitted against the work's own (no :fixed); with two, one increment has no shape, so the :fixed taken off is bounded by what the smallest size leaves beyond its estimated per-unit part; constant work's cost itself is fitted. Bounding :fixed alone (tried first) let a second pass whose per-unit estimate is loose at small n read as linear, so three sizes use the increments. Proof: a seon.cost-test case whose rows are another case's minus a constant (a speedup of the fixed part) gives the same steeper verdict, and the reach-update rows above are not steeper.", :changed #{"seon.cost/growth" "docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged" "seon.cost/judge"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#cost-proofs 33%: **Cost is work done (ticks, bytes, calls), never wall or CPU time** (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I like ticks vs wall clock or anything that is load dependent. Work done, not scheduled time slices\")." "docs/cost.md#how-we-measure 21%: Every measurement, in a cost proof or outside one, starts from a written estimate and is read across sizes, never from one quick run (owner: \"think about things algorithmically first and always put estimates on things\"):" "docs/agent.md#done 21%: Your task is done when every proof passes and the done rule refuses nothing; the proofs run after each of your replies. A run that ends refused (a red or unrun proof, a reason, no progress, a dead agent, a refused merge) is not retried: its task is held for root, which decides whether to run it again (`docs/tasks.md#a-refused-run-is-held-for-root`)." "docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract 11%: A function's contract may carry its cost bound beside its schema: `:seon.cost/bound`, a plain function of the same arguments answering what one call may cost, `{:ticks :bytes :calls}` (any of them) and `:out`, the measure of its answer that a caller's bound passes on to the next callee (```seon.cost/bound``` reads it)." "docs/config.md#constants 10%: Every limit, bound or default is a config key (AGENTS §5)."], :functions [["seon.cost/growth 25%: Dimension d's growth over rows under estimate e, {:exponent :allowed :basis}, or nil when\n  fewer than two sizes measured it: the fitted exponent and the one the estimate's growth\n  allows over the same sizes (its own plus steeper-by). A change making every size cheaper,\n  or dearer, by one amount never moves it (task seon.cost/a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper;\n  docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged). The basis: :increments, each size's increment over the\n  smallest against the work's own (three sizes or more with two positive increments, the\n  largest at least growth-share of the smallest size's cost, no fixed part assumed);\n  :total, the cost itself, for constant work (it should not grow);\n  else :bounded-fixed, the estimate's :fixed taken off but never more than the smallest\n  size leaves beyond its estimated per-unit part. On the stated :fixed alone, a faster fixed\n  part left the smallest size's remainder near 0 and the fit read the speedup as growth." [:seon.cost/dim :seon.cost/row :seon.cost/sized]] "seon.cost/growth-share 11%: Growth is fitted on increments over the smallest size only when the largest increment is at least this share of the smallest size's cost: below it, the measure's own variance moves the increments more than the growth does." "seon.cost/steeper-by 7%: A dimension is steeper when its fitted exponent exceeds its growth's own exponent over the same sizes by more than this: n log n under a linear estimate (+0.17 over 100x) passes, a second loop over the data (+1.0) does not." "seon.cost/exact 7%: The dimensions a verdict is decided on: counted, so the same code on the same input measures the same on any machine at any load, and a green is a function of its reach digest (AGENTS.md section 5, results reused by input digest)." "seon.cost/ratchet 5%: An accepted overrun stays green while every dimension at the largest size is within this multiple of what was measured when its task was filed (Seon's ratchet: the spread of one operation measured apart), so a regression is red even inside an open overrun."], :schemas [[:seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum "17%"] [:seon.cost/sized "14%"] [:seon.cost/row "10%"] [:seon.config.proofs/case-sizes "8%"] [:seon.config.proofs/cost-settled "6%"]]}}
{:task "seon.main/docstrings-carry-no-dates-and-no-old-words", :plan "What exists: docstrings in `src` carry dated rulings and sessions' provenance, which git keeps (docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept: nothing is a dated log; AGENTS §7: a docstring says what a var is). 24 `src` files hold an ISO date: `seon.cost` (`units`, `tick-allowance`, `ratchet`: measurement dates), `seon.program` (the reach-index docstring near :1085), `seon.agent` (`run-tests-forked`, the test-plan docstring near :456), `seon.reconcile` (`hold-tx`, near :444), `seon.db` (`collect!`: \"owner, 2026-10-22, simplest first\"), `seon.template` (`with-merge`: \"pure-tests 2026-10-23\"), `seon.check` and others. `seon.root/start!`'s docstring calls `:stall-ms` \"a guard\" (AGENTS §9: a guard is the old word, and the stall check is the clock task `seon.root/one-clock`'s). Correct: each docstring states the rule as it holds, the owner's ruling cited as \"DECISIONS, <title>\" where it explains the rule; the measurement kept without its date; `:stall-ms` described as the stall bound it is. Deletes the dated clauses. A law proves it: no docstring of a `src` var holds an ISO date (read from the compiler's facts, `:var/doc`), so a new one is red.", :changed #{"seon.context/current-state" "seon.db/collect!" "seon.fork-server/server-step" "seon.root/start!" "seon.cost/units" "seon.problems/run-rows" "seon.reconcile/done-explain" "seon.db/delete-main!" "seon.agent/base-graph" "seon.cost/sizes" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "seon.task/carry-tx" "my.root/gate!" "seon.merge/merge-tx" "my.schema/forms" "seon.program/process-tests" "seon.fork-server/main" "seon.template/with-merge" "seon.cost/tick-allowance" "seon.agent/test-plan" "seon.program/banned" "seon.cost/exact" "seon.writer/writer-proc" "seon.reconcile/hold-tx" "seon.program/load-diff!" "seon.program/quoted-vars" "seon.agent/transact!" "seon.web/web-proc" "seon.config/sources" "my.schema/new-key-file" "seon.db/seed!" "my.agent/deferred" "seon.reconcile/actions" "seon.program/divergence" "seon.fx/test-command" "seon.agent-proc/stall-ms" "seon.program/env-readers" "seon.task/accumulate" "seon.agent/run-selection" "seon.config/refuse!"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/health.md#a-stale-section 45%: A wiki section that cites code is stale when what it describes of that code changed after its text: a cited var's contract or docstring (`seon.problems/stale-sections`, class `:stale-section`; owner, 2026-10-25, DECISIONS Dogfood rulings). A body that changed while both stayed is not a change of what the section describes. When the loader writes a section's text it records the form id of each var the text cites (`:seon.section/read`, `seon.docs/read-map`; a form id is the form's own digest, so it moves exactly when the form's text does), and while the text is unchanged a reload keeps what was recorded: it is the code as it stood when the section was last written. A cited var whose form id now differs has its `:var/contract` and `:var/doc` facts read as of the transaction that wrote the section's read (or its review) and compared with today's: a contract or docstring change is itself a transaction (`seon.program/def-tx` asserts and retracts them, history kept), so no digest of meaning is stored. The row names the section, the var, what changed (`:changed`), what it was (`:was`) and is (`:now`). The eval that makes such a change says so in its response (`:redefined`, `seon.program/meaning-changes` over its own transaction), with the sections citing the var, so the agent sees the change it made and what to update. A form id unchanged since the read cannot have changed what it says, so only the names whose form moved read history (the before and after on main are in the commit that made this the rule). It closes when the section is edited, which re-reads the code, or when someone has read it as it is and says so with `my.docs/reviewed!` (who, and the form ids it read at; the older reviews stay in the history), with the text untouched. The comparison is by value, never by tx: a branch's tx counter is its own." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 17%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check." "docs/vocabulary.md#tasks-task-chains-words 15%: The words for tasks and their prerequisites, and the namespace that owns each." "docs/decisions.md#patterns-live-in-the-topic-sections-linked 13%: Owner, 2026-10-21: \"I want us to learn how we can detect patterns in our code and reuse these solutions\"." "docs/source.md#mains-program-follows-the-tree 11%: Main's program is the tree's: at each boot, when the files read differently from main's last import (their digest differs from the fact's `:digest`, or none is recorded), main's program is replaced by the tree's in one transaction, ```seon.source/replace-tx```; when they read the same, nothing is imported or written."], :functions [["seon.program/meaning 9%: What a definition says of itself that text describing it relies on, so what a wiki section citing it goes stale on (seon.problems/stale-sections) and an eval that changes it shows (meaning-changes): its contract and its docstring, the facts def-tx asserts and retracts (DECISIONS Dogfood rulings; the contract carries the arity)." [:var/contract :var/doc]] ["my.docs/reviewed! 5%: Record that who read section address (docs/x.md#heading) as it is, with the code it cites as it stands now: its recorded form ids become the current ones, its text is not touched, and the review (who, and the form ids it read at) is kept as a fact on this branch." [:seon.config.source/branch]] ["seon.problems/stale-sections 5%: Each section whose text cites a var whose contract or docstring changed since the section was last written or reviewed, as a :stale-section row naming what changed (:changed), what it was as of then (:was) and what it is (:now): the var's facts (seon.program/meaning) as of the transaction that wrote the section's :seon.section/read (seon.docs/load-tx) or its :seon.section/reviewed (my.docs/reviewed!), read from history, against now." [:seon.problems/row :seon.section/address :seon.section/baseline-unread :seon.section/read :seon.section/reviewed :var/contract :var/doc :var/name]] ["seon.root/import-source! 5%: Main's program follows Seon's own source (seon.source): root's dirs read through the :source effect (first brought to the main checkout's branch, seon.fx/read-source: fast-forwarded when behind, a recorded gap when it holds commits main lacks; their files, the tree's HEAD commit and its reference-code pins, and the tree's inputs with no program facts, seon.fx/inputs)." [:form/id :ns/file :schema/file :seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/source]] ["seon.agent/test-plan 4%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]]], :schemas [[:seon.problems/class "9%"] [:seon.config.root/wiki-baseline "8%"] [:seon.config.agent/stall-ms "6%"] [:var/doc "5%"] [:seon.config.source/tests-dir "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.check/health-is-what-a-branch-caused", :plan "Root's ruling, 2026-10-23, on the walk session's finding (the owner asked: \"why is health not useful? that's a problem\"). What happens: in a worktree whose config keys differ from main's, `bin/check health` exits 1 with no output and no verdict line. Two causes stack. bin/worktree copies main's target/seon, compiled with main's config schema, so it refuses the tree's resources/seon/config/default.edn (\"wiki-baseline missing-key, turn-text-budget unknown-key\"), and nothing compares target/seon.tree with the tree it runs on. Then bin/check's single mode runs `run seed 2000 > /dev/null || exit 1`, so its error is thrown away (AGENTS section 9: every check ends on X OK / X FAILED). And even when it runs, the step prints the tree's absolute open rows (seon.problems/health), which were red on an unchanged main when it was added (10 dead citations, 01fd0496), while a Seon agent is held only to what its branch caused since its merge base (seon.problems/caused, the done rule). Build: (1) bin/check never hides a step: a failed seed or drop prints its output and MODE FAILED. A driver mode of the binary (seon.main) refuses, saying which tree it was built from and which it runs on, when target/seon.tree is not the checkout's tree (seon.fx/tree-id), rather than checking with a stale binary. (2) The health step is what a branch caused: seon.problems/caused-split of the base against the checkout. The base is the branch's merge base with git's main; with main checked out and a clean tree, its first parent (the previous main, so a merge that adds problems is red); with main checked out and uncommitted changes, HEAD (seon.fx/base-commit). The base db is the head's with the base tree's program, wiki and tasks loaded by root's own loaders (seon.source/replace-tx, seon.docs/load-dir-tx, seon.task/load-tx), from the base tree extracted by git (seon.fx/checkout-at). Its result is keyed by the tree and the base's tree (seon.proofs/step-digests), so an unchanged pair reuses it. bin/laws catch reads the same step for :unnamed-site. Absolute health stays root's to show live (root's context and page lead with it); the merge session reads it through the same step with the previous main as base, so there is no second path. AGENTS section 3's \"run bin/check health\" lines say so. Deletes: the absolute verdict of bin/check health.", :changed #{"seon.fx/checkout-at" "docs/checks.md#modes" ":seon.config.source/main" "docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon" "seon.proofs/check-steps" "seon.fx/base-commit" "seon.fx/tree-of" "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "seon.fx/tree-id" "seon.proofs/step-digests" "seon.problems/caused-split"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon 76%: `bin/check health [CLASS ...]` (`seon.check/health-check`) is the one check of these classes, and it holds a checkout to what a Seon agent is held to: what its branch caused (root's ruling, 2026-10-23). It boots Seon on the checkout's source and task files and loads the wiki (the head). It then loads the base commit's tree onto that db with root's own loaders (the base). The base commit is ```seon.fx/base-commit```: with a branch checked out, its merge base with git's main (`:seon.config.source/main`), so a branch with no commits of its own caused nothing; with main checked out and a clean tree, the previous main, so the merge session's run on a merged main judges the merge; with main checked out and uncommitted changes, HEAD. The step judges `seon.problems/caused-split` of the two, the classes `seon.problems/caused-classes` names (a class it does not read is refused, never a green check), the done rule's own reading (```seon.check/branch-health```). It prints each row the branch caused (and `attention` rows, the sections it changed under the wiki, which never fail it), and how many rows the head holds open in all, and ends `HEALTH OK` or `HEALTH FAILED`. A problem the base already had is not the branch's: it is root's to show, live, in its context and on its page. `bin/prove` keys the step by the tree and the base's tree (`seon.proofs/step-digests`), so an unchanged pair reuses its result. `bin/laws catch` calls it for `:unnamed-site`; it checks nothing of its own. Not this: a second checker over the files (`bin/check docs` beside the class), or the tree's absolute open rows as a branch's verdict (red on an unchanged main, so every session learned to read past it)." "docs/checks.md#modes 54%: A step is a mode of `target/seon` (except `laws`, which is `bin/laws`, `lmdb-kill` and `lmdb-sem`, native programs on LMDB envs of their own, and `forks`, `bin/forks`: the forks' own suites on Jolt, [forks](forks.md#own-suites)), its body over one harness that boots root, runs it, and ends on the one verdict line `NAME OK` or `NAME FAILED` with its exit status (`seon.check/run-check`, [machine](machine.md#builds-and-checks)). The harness is a driver, not the program: `seon.check` lives in `test/seon/check.clj`, outside src's count (task `seon.main/drivers-leave-src`). The binary's main is ```seon.main/-main```: it serves `root` and `ports` itself and, for every other mode, loads the driver from the checkout's source at run time (its deps.edn roots added to the loader, `test/` and `src/` to the source roots), so the release binary holds only the program (220.4 MB, against 238.1 MB with the driver compiled in) and a check process starts about 0.7 s later (1.7 s for `steps`, cached, against 1.0 s). `bin/check` runs no binary built from another tree: `bin/build` records the tree it built from (`target/seon.tree`, named by `bin/tree`, as ```seon.fx/tree-id``` names it), and a run or a mode whose checkout is another tree is refused, naming both, with `MODE FAILED (stale binary)` (`seon.check-test/a-stale-binary-is-refused`). The tree is the whole checkout's less the paths `:seon.config.source/tree-excludes` names (`notes/`: working papers no read and no build reads, so a merge-log line moves no digest and rebuilds nothing), so any other edit, a wiki page included, asks for `bin/build seon` before the next single mode; `bin/prove` builds first whenever a step it selects runs the binary, so its runs are never refused. A worktree starts with main's binary copied, which refused a worktree's config whose keys differed before it said anything. A mode's seed and drop are quiet when they work; when one fails, its own output and `MODE FAILED (seed)` (or `(drop)`) are printed (`seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed`): no step ends without its verdict line." "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant 26%: The failure points whose edge function catches, each with the one variant it declares and who handles it as data." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 25%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check. The merge's proof is `bin/prove` on the merged tree, then `bin/check smoke`; `bin/laws` (every law's sites) and `health` ([health](health.md)) run inside it as steps keyed by the whole tree. One rule selects for everyone: a key runs exactly when no green result is stored at its reach digest (```seon.proofs/plan```), in the one results directory every checkout shares (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`), so an unchanged tree runs nothing and a result proven in any checkout is reused in every other." "docs/agent.md#what-you-read 22%: Your context comes in two parts: the run's, sent once and unchanged while you work, then the turn's, measured again after each of your replies."], :functions ["seon.proofs/check-steps 6%: bin/check's steps, the one list of them (bin/check reads it from ./seon steps and fails a step it does not name), as prove keys them: each step name (a bin/check --steps unit) to the seon.check mode functions its body runs, whose reach is the step's (seon.program/ reach-digests with roots), with the checkout's :env (bin/, native/, resources/, the pins: the binary's other inputs) in every digest." ["seon.root/import-source! 6%: Main's program follows Seon's own source (seon.source): root's dirs read through the :source effect (first brought to the main checkout's branch, seon.fx/read-source: fast-forwarded when behind, a recorded gap when it holds commits main lacks; their files, the tree's HEAD commit and its reference-code pins, and the tree's inputs with no program facts, seon.fx/inputs)." [:form/id :ns/file :schema/file :seon.error/message :seon.error/subject :seon.root/id :seon.root/source]] "seon.contract/checked 5%: f checked by contract form c (compiled once, here: seon.contract/wrap), errors named fn-sym; f itself when instrumentation is off." ["seon.contract/on? 5%: Are the contracts on in this process (:seon.config.contract/instrument)? :off: no wrapper and no recording; :contracts: wrappers, no recording transaction (for measuring the two apart); :on: both." [:seon.config.contract/instrument]] ["seon.problems/stale-sections 5%: Each section whose text cites a var whose contract or docstring changed since the section was last written or reviewed, as a :stale-section row naming what changed (:changed), what it was as of then (:was) and what it is (:now): the var's facts (seon.program/meaning) as of the transaction that wrote the section's :seon.section/read (seon.docs/load-tx) or its :seon.section/reviewed (my.docs/reviewed!), read from history, against now." [:seon.problems/row :seon.section/address :seon.section/baseline-unread :seon.section/read :seon.section/reviewed :var/contract :var/doc :var/name]]], :schemas [[:seon.config.source/main "32%"] [:seon.config.source/tree-excludes "30%"] [:seon.problems/class "17%"] [:seon.task/state "6%"] [:seon.config.source/gate-checkout "5%"]]}}
{:task "seon.reconcile/the-judge-reads-its-branch-in-one-request", :plan "Done (2026-10-23, shrink): the judge asks the writer once; :branch-diff's answer carries the head db, its merge base and main's t there (seon.writer, from the base branch-merge already read), and the :read step and its arm went; judge-tx takes that one answer. Was: after :tested, `seon.reconcile/advance` asked the writer :branch-db (step :read), then :branch-diff (step :diffed), and judge-tx read both. The writer's :branch-diff already reads the branch head (`seon.writer/branch-merge` answers [head result]). Correct: :branch-diff's answer carries the head db and base t; the judge asks once and the :read step goes. Deletes one round trip per run and the :read arm.", :changed #{"seon.writer/other-op" "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging" "seon.reconcile/judge-tx" "seon.reconcile/advance"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 10%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check." "docs/merging.md#the-test-service 9%: One proc, `:prove` (```seon.prove/prove-proc```, declared in `resources/seon/system.edn`), admits every request to run tests, whoever asks: a Claude session (the server's `:prove` op, the MCP tool `seon_prove`, ```seon.root/prove!```) and an agent's turn (```seon.agent/prove!```, which ```seon.turn/observe``` calls) share it, so a change is proved once." "docs/vocabulary.md#tasks-task-chains-words 6%: The words for tasks and their prerequisites, and the namespace that owns each." "docs/storage.md#merges-read-what-changed 6%: A merge decides what merges from the datoms that differ, never by reading whole programs." "docs/root.md#runs-and-judging 5%: A run is one agent's try at a task; the reconciler starts it, and judges its end from measured facts only."], :functions [["seon.writer/other-op 6%: Every request but :tx." [:seon.config.store/sole-writer? :seon.root/collected :seon.root/id :seon.writer/request :seon.writer/state]] ["seon.reconcile/judge-tx 5%: Run aid's end on main from MEASURED facts: the end its agent wrote on the branch, root's own proof results, the branch's merge entries, and diff, the writer's one read of the branch (:branch-diff: its head db, its merge base and main's t there, the keys it changed, its unmerged keys, its contract gaps); the task content the branch changed carried to main, or its conflicts with main's recorded on the run for root (seon.task/carry-tx); and a failure it marked (my.task/fail!) carried with the task held." [:seon.error/message :seon.proof/results :seon.reconcile/state :seon.run/costs :seon.run/error :seon.run/failures :seon.run/taken :seon.run/task-conflicts :seon.run/waste :seon.task/execution :seon.task/failure :seon.task/id]] ["seon.writer/octopus-merge 5%: Merge branches into main in ONE commit (an octopus merge, session R3, as git's, every member a parent): each member that holds main's program head and is not refused merges, in order; a member whose changes meet an earlier member's is behind (it merges main first)." [:seon.db/branch :seon.merge/behind :seon.merge/data-attrs :seon.writer/state]] "seon.agent/prove! 3%: Ask the test service (seon.prove) to prove this branch at its head, and wait for its answer: the run's (run-tests-forked's answer, with :key)." ["seon.reconcile/take-answer 3%: [st requests] with answer m taken by the flight step that asked it (advance), and the next pass when that ended its flight." [:seon.reconcile/state]]], :schemas [[:seon.reconcile/step "19%"] [:seon.merge/result "14%"] [:seon.writer/state "6%"] [:seon.config.source/main "3%"] [:seon.db/tx-report "3%"]]}}
{:task "seon.main/dead-code-goes", :plan "Done (2026-10-23, shrink): seon.writer's :branch-info op had no sender since `seon.root/branch-info` went, and its arm went; `seon.reconcile/reconcile` (= (actions (index db))) and `seon.reconcile/running` (= (:live idx)) were test-only and went from src (seon.reconcile-test keeps reconcile as its own helper; the other tests call `seon.reconcile/actions` and `index` and read :live); `seon.source/file-ns` and `file-requires` read a file's first form once (`seon.source/ns-form`), and load-order reads each file once. Re-grepped when done: `seon.program/var-kind` was already gone, and `seon.merge/changed-vars` stays: seon.agent's test plan calls it.", :changed #{"seon.writer/other-op" "seon.source/file-ns" "seon.source/load-order" "docs/vocabulary.md#tasks-task-chains-words" "seon.source/ns-form"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/source.md#mains-program-follows-the-tree 55%: Main's program is the tree's: at each boot, when the files read differently from main's last import (their digest differs from the fact's `:digest`, or none is recorded), main's program is replaced by the tree's in one transaction, ```seon.source/replace-tx```; when they read the same, nothing is imported or written. So a resumed main follows its tree when worktree sessions merge and the tree moves on in git, or the next generation's tree holds Seon's own merges commit. Re-importing changed files alone would not do: a form's identity is its text (`seon.program/account-tx`), so a changed file's new forms would join its old ones, and a removed function would stay live. `replace-tx` is ```seon.source/import-tx```, read against main without the forms of the imported namespaces (each form takes its file's order and lead), with every program entity of those namespaces and of the schema resources that the import no longer makes retracted: a changed function's old form, a removed function's form and var, a removed file's namespace. Data (tasks, runs, turns, config and root facts) and the program of a namespace no file holds (an example program, a namespace an agent made) are untouched; history keeps the old program. A form or schema entry main holds already keeps its `:form/source` as it is, so a form merged in (which the binary does not hold) is still loaded at boot, and a form new to main is the binary's. The digest is of what was read (the files and the reference-code pins), not of the commit alone, so uncommitted edits in the tree count, and a merges commit made by `update-ref` under a checked-out tree (its files unchanged) imports nothing." "docs/health.md#cost 24%: Measured on 2026-10-23 (`seon.problems-test/dead-citations-follow-the-citations`, every citation dead, the worst case): 68 us, 68 KB and 4 contracted calls per cited name at n=1000, against an estimate of 90 us, 170 KB and 6. A citation that names something costs one set lookup. On root's own store (152 sections, 1,280 vars), a set built from main's var names and read for 1,712 names took 0.37 ms, against 18 ms for one AVET seek per name; that is why the class reads a set." "docs/storage.md#compiled-code 24%: The store holds definitions (`:form/src`), never compiled code: a process gets its program compiled by inheriting it (an agent forks the fork server's image, copy-on-write) or by loading forms it lacks: ```seon.program/load-all!``` (every live form a db has that the binary lacks), ```seon.program/load-changes!``` (the forms and removals a merge's resolved tx-data carry, which ```seon.agent/merge-main!``` loads right after its merge commit) and ```seon.program/load-diff!``` (the forms one db has over another, and the removals), and evaluations are records that nothing re-runs. A real form loads in 1.6–5 ms; Jolt's compiled units are 15× their source, hold absolute paths and are valid only under the runtime that compiled them, so they are not stored (task `seon.program/compiled-units-in-the-store` holds the idea). A branch's program at commit C is `d/commit-as-db` at C (0.7–0.9 ms) and `seon.program/load-diff!` to it, from definitions only: code and data definitions restore exactly; host cells (atoms, delays) come back empty and host objects (LMDB, sockets, procs, foreign libraries) are made fresh, as at boot." "docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach 15%: A test runs only when something it reaches changed since it was last green, in any checkout (task `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`)." "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches 12%: A merge runs what the change reaches, not every check."], :functions [["seon.agent/run-tests-forked 4%: Fork this agent; the child runs what test-plan runs for proofs (the selected tests with no green held at their digest), each test within ticks engine ticks of work of its own, writing each test var as it starts and then one EDN line of its result on a pipe." [:seon.error/message :seon.test/fork-refused :seon.test/result]] "seon.source/load-order 4%: The Clojure files ({relative path text}) with an ns form, as [ns path text] in the order loading them takes: each after the files it requires, ties by path (the order import-tx analyzes them in, so a namespace the process never loaded is set up before a file that requires it)." ["seon.reconcile/advance 4%: Run aid's flight takes answer r of its step." [:seon.agent/died :seon.agent/failed :seon.error/message :seon.error/operation :seon.error/type :seon.merge/behind :seon.proof/status :seon.proof/test :seon.reconcile/state :seon.reconcile/step :seon.root/ended :seon.root/no-agent :seon.root/no-source-text :seon.run/changed :seon.run/commit-error :seon.run/deferred-results :seon.run/end-ms :seon.run/error :seon.run/gate-unread :seon.run/id :seon.run/merged-t :seon.run/outcome :seon.run/reason :seon.run/status :seon.task/kind]] ["seon.agent/test-plan 3%: What judges branch db, as this process holds it: seon.proofs/plan of its tests' reach digests (seon.program/reach-digests) against base's (main at its merge base), the greens stored at those digests (files in results-dir, and the test service's result facts in db, greens and reds: seon.proofs/stored-results) and proofs." [:form/src :ns/form :ns/name :seon.config.source/test-results-dir :var/form :var/kind :var/name]] ["seon.problems/caused-split 3%: {:caused :attention :rows}: the derived problems the branch at head has that base (its merge base) had not, by message (:rows: each with its class, both kinds): what the branch's own work broke (:caused: the done rule refuses while any is open, seon.reconcile/done-explain :open-problem) and what it changed under the wiki (:attention: the sections it made stale, which the agent reads and never a reason to refuse; the classes :seon.problems/attention declares)." [:seon.merge/changes :seon.problems/class]]], :schemas [[:seon.task/state "5%"] [:seon.config.source/process-vars "5%"] [:seon.reconcile/actions "5%"] [:seon.merge/changes "5%"] [:seon.config.source/binary-vars "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.turn/a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget", :plan "Found by worktree dry-run's turn profile (2026-10-23, main 8a901ac3, temporary marks, reverted) and the diagnostics timing audit (notes/timing-audit.md, main checkout): every cost that made a scripted e2e-loop turn 17.7 s against a ~100 ms target grew without any record or check seeing it. Measured spans: test-plan's reach walk 4.3 s (5 plans a turn); merge-main's three-way 4.4 s; the writer's branch-diff 2.6 s (blocking the writer); the octopus merge ~1.6 s; problems/caused 0.37 s; a fresh root's first fork queued 3.8-4.8 s behind the fork server's replay of the boot import (d/with 3,504 ms for 42,942 datoms, then load-changes! 420 ms), visible only as that fork's queue time. What exists: an eval records `:ms` and `:alloc-bytes` (`seon.repl/wrap-cost`), no ticks or CPU; a turn stores `:seon.turn/split` {:context-ms :model-ms :eval-ms :record-ms :tests-ms}, rendered nowhere; spawn, merge, branch-diff, a Flow step and the fork server's replay record no measure as a fact. Correct (AGENTS §5, docs/cost.md: work is ticks, bytes and calls, exact; wall and CPU are measured, recorded, never red), re-scoped by the owner's ruling of 2026-10-23 in docs/decisions.md (the model sees each eval's cost, stable across turns so the prompt cache holds: recorded once, shown from that fact; no reused/computed marker): (1) DONE, every eval item's record carries its ticks (`jolt.host/ticks-of`), with :ms, :alloc-bytes and :fns, with :ms, :alloc-bytes and :fns, the values the recording stores (`seon.repl/wrap-cost`, `record-tx`) and the reply shows (`seon.repl/eval-string`), never measured again. Items (2) the turn's split in the context against an estimate, and (3) the fork server's replay, a spawn, merge-main, branch-diff and the octopus merge recording datoms, ticks and ms as a fact, are not taken here: (2) the context's rendering of a cost belongs to the agent-view render, (3) stays a facts-for-the-owner task to file on its own if wanted. Proofs: an eval's record carries :ticks, and the reply item and the stored record carry the same cost values (the render's proof of a stable rendering is the agent-view render's).", :changed #{"seon.repl/item-keys" "seon.repl/eval-string" "seon.repl/wrap-cost" "docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used" ":seon.cluster.eval/agent" "seon.repl/record-tx"}, :shown {:sections ["docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used 60%: An agent's task names its cost proofs as goals ([agent](agent.md)); `bin/test` runs them with every other test; a proof's `:ops` are priced in ticks, bytes and time from the unit table `seon.cost/units`, which `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` measures again and holds to 1.25x. Clocks that order events are `seon.profile/now-ns` ([machine](machine.md#the-clock)); `cpu-ns` orders nothing ([clocks](clocks.md))." "docs/cost.md#an-accepted-overrun-and-its-ratchet 33%: Cost is work done, counted (ticks, bytes, calls), never wall time (owner's ruling). A proof past its estimate stays green while the task its `:overrun` names is open and every dimension at the largest size is within `seon.cost/ratchet` (1.5x) of the `:measured` the task recorded, so a regression is red even inside an open overrun. A record is comparable only on the measure it was taken with: the proof's own thread's bytes and calls, exact and the same in every run, and that thread's CPU, which still moves with the machine's load. When the measure changes, every record is measured again on the new one, and its comment says when and under what load." "docs/cost.md#what-is-measured 27%: A proof measures the function under test, on the thread that calls it, and nothing else in the process (task `seon.cost/a-proof-measures-only-its-own-thread`):" "docs/decisions.md#open-owner-questions-pending-rulings-the-plans-blockers-are-also-in-planmd 27%: The questions waiting on the owner's ruling, the plan's blockers among them." "docs/clocks.md#time-measured-recorded-never-compared-to-decide 23%: Clock reads whose value is recorded or shown and never compared to decide anything."], :functions ["seon.cost/exact 16%: The dimensions a verdict is decided on: counted, so the same code on the same input measures the same on any machine at any load, and a green is a function of its reach digest (AGENTS.md section 5, results reused by input digest)." ["seon.turn/act! 5%: One model turn: the context of what was observed to the model, the reply's eval calls through the one REPL path, each answered as its own result, the turn's facts on the branch." [:seon.config.ai/cache-block-tokens :seon.run/id :seon.task/id :seon.turn/basis :seon.turn/calls :seon.turn/error :seon.turn/errors :seon.turn/finish :seon.turn/forms :seon.turn/id :seon.turn/model :seon.turn/ms :seon.turn/n :seon.turn/origin :seon.turn/reasoning :seon.turn/red :seon.turn/reply :seon.turn/run :seon.turn/sent :seon.turn/split :seon.turn/tokens :seon.turn/truncated]] "seon.repl/middleware 4%: Outermost first." "seon.profile/measure! 4%: f called and measured, its value answered: the engine ticks it ran (the work it did, the exact measure: jolt.host/ticks-of arms the tick timer, and the count is read in a finally inside it, so a call that throws is counted), the bytes this thread allocated, its wall ns (now-ns) and its thread's CPU ns (cpu-ns), and the contracted calls of the epoch it opens (begin!)." ["seon.turn/context-block 4%: The request of task as a model is sent it on this branch now (the proofs run again): what an outside agent working the task reads, the system prompt and its page, as one text, recorded as a turn of the run (origin :outside)." [:seon.run/id :seon.task/id :seon.turn/basis :seon.turn/id :seon.turn/n :seon.turn/origin :seon.turn/run :seon.turn/sent :seon.turn/split]]], :schemas [[:seon.turn/split "18%"] [:seon.config.agent/form-top-fns "12%"] [:seon.config.agent/test-ticks "5%"] [:seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd "5%"] [:seon.eval/response "4%"]]}}
{:task "seon.render/walk", :plan "The one mechanism every view of Seon's data goes through: the owner's window, root's page and the model's context (owner, 2026-10-23: \"locations should be based on a starting EID and we pull from there to render the data what one hop away\"; \"I want logical solution for everything so we aren't inventing new shit\"; \"rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"). What v1 did (~/src/seon/resources/seon/schemas/seon.agent.edn :agent, render/walk.clj neighborhood at :seon.render/distance 1): an entity's schema declares the neighbours a view of it shows as :seon.render/units, attributes in pull's own syntax (a leading _ the reverse ref), components included; the walk pulls the entity and those attributes, and each neighbour renders by its own schema's render. Bring the rule, not v1's 1263 lines (most are SCI caps and caches). Build: (1) :seon.render/units on the schemas this needs: the agent's (:seon.agent/agent-fact: its run :seon.run/_agent, its evaluations :seon.eval/_agent, its root), the run's (:seon.run/run-fact: its task, its turns :seon.turn/_run, the messages to it :seon.message/_to), the task's (its plan, proofs), root's (:seon.root/root-fact: its agents :seon.agent/_root, the open problems); (2) seon.render/walk: one d/pull of an eid with its own attributes and its units (on a branch: the branch's db, or seon.union's U for what spans branches, ids issued once store-wide, docs/storage.md#ids), plus what its string attributes cite (seon.ref/parse, each citation pulled at the depth its delimiter asks, as seon.context pulls it), answering the tree as one map (owner, 2026-10-23: \"We are returning a giant map right? so we can show what rendered it, what it is (a doc a symbol to a file, etc ?)\"): each node its value and what it is (an entity and its schema, a var, a doc section path#anchor, a file path:line, a citation found in a string), where it came from (eid and attribute, or the string and citation that pulled it) and the render that showed it with its :as, so an agent reading it knows what to change: the producer, the render or the citation. Every key in that map is fully namespaced and has a registered schema (owner, 2026-10-23: \"we only use fully namespaced schemas and we don't want to be cute with :you stuff\"): a pulled entity keeps its own attributes, and the walk's own parts are schemas of seon.render (its references, each citation's place, which render made which part). A citation's content is pulled once per context and cited by its number where it appears, so two citations of one var never repeat it; What each kind of value the walk pulls costs and who needs it (owner via root, 2026-10-23: \"I want each piece of data we are processing to be looked at and determined if we should just be dumping the whole thing in without regard to whether it's relevant\"), measured as the model's text on one run (the /walk root, task seon.render/config-shows-where-each-value-came-from), before -> after: root's config (its 108 :seon.config/* attributes, a 14 KB value) 1,801 -> 0 B: nobody needs it whole, the model needs the keys its task's code reads; root's focused render (seon.render.ai/root-fact) shows its boot, generation and how it last stopped, and a config key reaches a context only as one the task's code reads (to build); root 87 B, one line; an agent 386 -> 94 B: status, pid, branch and the form running (seon.render.ai/agent-fact), its description only for an agent no run started, cut at the producer (seon.root/observed: a run's agent's :task was its task id, which pulled the whole task into every walk of the agent a second time, 375 B -> 0); a run 753 -> 159 B: id, status or outcome and reason, model, turn of max, branch (seon.render.ai/run-fact); its plan is its task's and its boot root's, both cut; a task 906-1,265 B, its plan 263 B and proofs 53 B: shown whole, the model's work is in them; a cited var at its citation's depth 605 B: whole, the depth chooses it. Totals: an agent's walk 3,920 -> 945 B; root's 2,594 -> 703 B; a run's 2,589 -> 2,042 B. Not on this run, to measure when seon.context/evaluations records them: turns, evaluations, messages, problems, doc sections, schema forms. Calls for the owner: whether config shows the keys the task's code reads (a reach over the :seon.config/* keywords its forms name) or only a key a text cites; whether a task as a run's neighbour is whole or its plan and proofs only; root's walk shows every boot's agents (two for one run after a restart): this boot's only, or ended ones collapsed into one line. prose is markdown and facts are data (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Somethings are better as data and some are better as markdown\"): a value whose schema is prose (help, a plan, a doc section, a message, a reply) renders as markdown and is parsed for its citations; an entity, proofs, cost, the references and the provenance render as data an agent can read back and use; each value's render decides by its schema, so one evaluation may hold both; (3) the tree printed by the one printer (seon.print with seon.render/nested): the root of the walk by its :html render :as :block, each unit :as :tile; a page part is drawn by its :html render alone, never its :ai face (task a-page-part-renders-only-its-view). What is not useful is cut at its producer or given a focused render on its schema, never filtered by a view (owner: \"if shit isn't useful we either cut it at the producer or make specific renders to help focus the context or html\"). Read first: docs/render.md, seon.render (render-of, nested, print-ai), seon.print, seon.ref, seon.context's pulls (what it pulls today and at which depth), v1's walk. Replaces nothing yet: page and evaluations delete the hand-built producers.", :changed #{":seon.attempt/attempt-fact" "seon.context/current-state" ":seon.root/root-fact" "seon.render/walk" ":seon.agent/description" "seon.docs/reached" ":seon.render/n" ":seon.render/ai-by" ":seon.render/html-by" "seon.docs/load-dir-tx